# Carl Berger: person entry draft (Canadian persons, Tier 1)

Prepared 2026-10-14. **This is a proposal for review only.** No atlas file was edited and nothing was
committed. The draft is [entry-draft.json](entry-draft.json); it was generated by a throwaway script in the
session scratchpad.

**Validation.** The draft was applied in memory to the live graph (revision 1.135: 169 nodes, 939 edges). The
in-memory application added the node, set `node_id`, added 7 new sources and 6 addenda, added edges `edge_940`–`edge_942`,
set two roster `edge_id`s and applied the optional `edge_824` relabel. The result has 170 nodes and 942 edges. It passes
`validate_graph` with **0 errors and the same 7 existing warnings**, and no new or resolved warnings. Every edge
source id resolves and every `target_strand` exists.

## What is proposed

- **Node `carl_berger`.**
  - It has the twelve standard person keys.
  - Date label: *The Sense of Power 1970 · The Writing of Canadian History 1976; 2nd ed. 1986*.
  - The label lists dated works and has no "coverage through" tail. Person nodes carry no `coverage`.
- **`people_update`.** The existing record gets `node_id: carl_berger`.
- **Edge 1: → `cdn_intellectual_cultural` (contribution).** *Imperialism as nationalism, 1970*.
  - Strand `empire_and_nation`; roster row `cdn_intellectual_cultural`.
- **Edge 2: → `cdn_national_tradition` (contribution).** *The tradition as historiography, 1976–86*.
  - Strand `analysing_tradition`; roster row `cdn_national_tradition`.
- **Edge 3: → `cdn_national_tradition` (critique).** *Against the liberal nationalist reading of imperialism, 1970*.
  - Strand `liberal_conservative_nationalists`; no roster row.
  - It records his criticism, in his own words, of the "so-called liberal nationalist school". That school saw
    imperialism as a colonial past that "succumbed to Canadian nationalism" (1970, p. 9).
  - The validator raises no repeated-triple warning, because edges 2 and 3 have different types.
- **Optional relabel of `edge_824`.**
  - Current label: "Berger's historiography of English-Canadian historical writing". It is title-level and duplicates
    edge 2.
  - Proposed: historical writing treated as a domain of English-Canadian intellectual history (1971–86). The evidence is
    Trofimenkoff 1979 p. 9 and n. 4 (Cook's "La Survivance" essays), Karr 1989 p. 161, and Berger 1986 p. 261.
  - This follows the `edge_886`, `edge_063`, `edge_720` and `edge_742` precedents. The alternative is to leave it
    unchanged.
- **New sources** (`pe135_berger_*`):
  - `monet_1972`, `mills_1977` and `tcook_1977` (all read in full);
  - `cole_1970` (metadata only);
  - `cole_1971` (extract and notes);
  - `carr_1982` (abstract);
  - `owram_2013` (later reading, snippets).
- **Addenda:** `cic_berger_1970`, `cic_berger_1976`, `cic_trofimenkoff_1979`, `cic_karr_1989`, `qcreq_rudin_1997`
  and `wcreq_kealey_1995`.
  - The sibling Cook draft also adds a `cic_karr_1989` addendum. The applier keeps both notes.

## Sibling drafts

The other Tier 1 folders are cook, careless, creighton, parr and valverde. They were checked for overlap, and none of
them proposes an edge from Berger or revises `edge_824`. Some sibling edges share a target or strand with this draft,
but each carries a different claim:

| Sibling edge | Shared target or strand | Its claim |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Cook `rel_07` | `cdn_intellectual_cultural/empire_and_nation` (edge 1's strand) | Cook's own critique of nationalism |
| Cook `rel_03` | `cdn_national_tradition`, strand `laurentian` | A critique of the Laurentian view |
| Careless `rel_05` | `who_killed_debate` | The fragmentation lead (see "Not proposed" below) |

## What was read

Neither book could be read continuously: every Internet Archive copy is borrow-only, and the search-inside service
returned 403. Berger's own words therefore come from **Google Books snippet views**. These are search fragments with
page numbers, not continuous reading, and every note that uses them says so. Three in-period reviews were read in full
and corroborate them.

| Source | Check | Supports |
| --- | --- | --- |
| *Sense of Power*, 2013 printing (`-Y-BAAAAQBAJ`) | Snippets. The pagination equals 1970: Monet's pp. 132 and 261 citations match | "This book is a study in Canadian nationalist thought" (p. 3); "one variety of Canadian nationalism", the liberal nationalist school and Skelton (p. 9); method (p. 10); "Imperialism was one form of Canadian nationalism" (p. 259) |
| *Writing of Canadian History*, 1986 (`jRN6AAAAMAAJ`) and 1976 (`bix6AAAAMAAJ`) | Snippets | Both prefaces (scope, the eight figures, "self-conscious effort", the "two rather different 'nations'"); pp. 181, 261–262 (he quotes Careless 1969), 289, 298, 306–307, 312 |
| Monet, RHAF 26:2 (1972) 274–276 | Read in full (Érudit PDF) | Novelty; "très difficile, sinon impossible, à réfuter"; Penlington as precursor; the first critics (Page, Cole, Nelles) |
| Mills, CJPST 1:1 (1977) 134–137 | Read in full | Intellectual history; Berger's p. 259 quoted; "much too much weight on that over-used term" |
| Terry Cook, Archivaria 5 (1977–78) 198–203 | Read in full | "transcends historiography to become an intellectual history of English Canada" |
| Trofimenkoff (1979) | Re-read pp. 9–11 | "decidedly masculine affairs"; historical writing as "a domain of intellectual history" |
| Karr (1989) | Re-read pp. 160–168 | Group biography; "over-Canadianization"; Carr's "scavenger hunt for nationalism" |
| Rudin (1997) | Snippets pp. 3–6, 55, 58 | Rejects "united by their search for the truth"; Groulx and Dollard |
| Kealey (1995; Ryerson essay 1982) | Snippets pp. 18, 30, 84 | Ryerson "not considered for systematic evaluation"; accepts the 1986 labour summary |
| Cole, JBS (1971) | Extract and references only | n. 33, "the strongest case for a Canadian nationalistic consciousness" |
| Carr, JCS (1982) | Crossref abstract only | Revisionists "share many of the traditional views" |
| Owram (2013) | Snippets; a later reading | "pretty much the standard interpretation" |

## Not read

- **Both books, continuously.** The Sense of Power's p. 260 sentence on the "sense of power" was not used, because its
  opening was cut off in the snippet.
- **The 1976 conclusion** (pp. 259–264), except as Mills quotes it.
- **Behind paywalls or bot walls:**
  - Cole 1970 and Page 1970 (JCS);
  - Nelles 1970 (Canadian Forum);
  - Neary's CHR review (1978);
  - Heick (AHR 1977), Prang (IJ 1977), Bell (CJPS 1972), Preston (AHR 1972), Brunet (IJ 1971);
  - Tippett, CHR (1986);
  - Kaufmann (1998, two articles: one HTML-only, one 403);
  - Desmond Morton, RHAF (1983): Érudit's Anubis bot wall.
- **The body of Carr 1982** (its abstract was read).
- **Science, God, and Nature in Victorian Canada** (1983): metadata only.
- **Not used:** Kelebay (McGill Journal of Education, 1984) was read in full but not used. Désilets (RHAF 1971, on the
  1969 reader) was read but not used.

## Decisions

- **Male focus.** The brief asked about criticism of his focus on male, English-Canadian, central-Canadian historians.
  - No in-period text found here faults *Writing* for its all-male subjects. Trofimenkoff (1979) criticises the field and
    *Sense of Power*'s imperialists; the draft says so and does not overstate.
  - Mills names eight "superluminaries".
- **Quebec omission.** The draft presents it as Berger's stated choice (preface; p. 181) and records Rudin's in-period
  criticism.
- **Central Canada.** No regional critique of *Writing* was found.
- **Founder claims.** The draft makes none. Monet credits Penlington (1965) with suggesting the thesis.
- **Careless misattribution.** Terry Cook (1977) cites the "nation-building … neglects and obscures" passage as
  Berger's (p. 263). The 1986 text shows that Berger is quoting Careless (1969). The draft attributes it to Careless.
- **No citation counts in the text.** Citation counts are in `citation_evidence` only.

## Open questions for the editor

1. **Snippet witnesses.** Berger's own words rest on Google Books snippets, corroborated by full-text reviews. Is
   `supporting_page_checked`, with the snippet caveat in every note, acceptable? The other option is to keep
   `cic_berger_1976` at metadata level.
2. **Edge 3 strand.** Should edge 3 keep `liberal_conservative_nationalists`? Existing person→group critiques use a null
   strand. Berger names Skelton, who has no atlas entry, and not Lower or Underhill.
3. **The `edge_824` relabel.** Accept it, or leave it unchanged?
4. **Reported death.** Wikidata and Wikipedia report his death in October 2026, but no obituary was found. The entry says
   "b. 1939".
5. **Science, God, and Nature (1983).** It is unread. Should it stay only in the representative works?

No email, mailto or other personal identifier was sent to any service. The services used were the Wikidata MCP,
Crossref, OpenAlex (repo key), Open Library, id.loc.gov, the Internet Archive metadata, Google Books, Érudit, Cambridge
Core, Archivaria, UVic OJS, UBC OJS, UNB and web search.

