  "description": "An English-Canadian historian of nationalism, Quebec and Victorian thought. Attacking a nationalist manifesto in the centennial year, he suggested that 'instead of constantly deploring our lack of identity, we should attempt to understand and explain the regional, ethnic and class identities that we do have. It might just be that it is in these limited identities that “Canadianism” is found' (1967, as quoted by Buckner); Careless made 'limited identities' a programme in 1969. Cook did not make region his unit of analysis: reviewing regional syntheses, he doubted 'the existence of the phenomena we have for so long called regions' and judged that 'as a tool of analysis, “regionalism” is a concept whose time has gone' (1983). The Regenerators (1985) read late Victorian social critics as replacing 'man's salvation' with 'a concern for social salvation', so that Christianity became 'less rather than more relevant' (as quoted by Gauvreau); Michael Gauvreau answered in 1991 that the book rests on 'military metaphors' that recent studies had abandoned, and a 1998 review named Cook, with David Marshall, the strongest advocate of the 'secularization thesis'. His lecture 'Cabbages Not Kings' (1990) proposed an ecological reading of contact in which 'Cabbages, at least as much as kings, led to the transformation of the new world'. In the quarrel over national history he called the debate between 'nationalists' and 'limited identitarians' 'arid' (1993), and in 2000 he argued that identities are 'contingent' and 'multiple rather than single'.",
  "representative_figures_and_works": "Ramsay Cook (1931–2016); Canada and the French-Canadian Question (1966); “Canadian Centennial Cerebrations” (International Journal, 1967); The Maple Leaf Forever: Essays on Nationalism and Politics in Canada (1971); Canada 1896–1921: A Nation Transformed (1974, with Robert Craig Brown); “The Burden of Regionalism” (Acadiensis, 1977); “Regionalism Unmasked” (Acadiensis, 1983); The Regenerators: Social Criticism in Late Victorian English Canada (1985); “Cabbages Not Kings: Towards an Ecological Interpretation of Early Canadian History” (Journal of Canadian Studies, 1990–91); “The New History of Atlantic Canada” (Acadiensis, 1993); “‘Identities Are Not Like Hats’” (Canadian Historical Review, 2000). Readings: Careless (1969); Gaffield (1986); Karr (1989); Gauvreau (1991); Marshall (1993–94); Buckner (2000).",
