  "scope_note": "This entry rests on texts read on 2026-10-14: three of Cook's Acadiensis review essays in full (1977, 1983, 1993), the opening of the 1967 article (OpenAlex text) and the abstract of his 2000 forum piece. The limited-identities sentence (1967) and the 1970 essay reprinted in The Maple Leaf Forever are known through Buckner's (2000) and Karr's (1989) quotations. The Regenerators is unread: its words appear here as quoted by Gauvreau (1991) and Karr, with Gaffield's 1986 review and the book's contents. 'Cabbages Not Kings' was read for the environment drafts on 2026-10-13 and is not re-read here. Canada and the French-Canadian Question is unread. He is not presented as a founder: Buckner says Cook coined 'limited identities' and Careless popularized it, and Cook wrote in 1995 that the phrase was 'usually attributed to Maurice Careless and I have decided that he is welcome to it!' (quoted by Buckner). Contested in period: Gauvreau (1991) on The Regenerators and on limited identities as a frame that marginalized religion; Karr (1989) on his 'anti-national crusade'; Buckner (2000) on the additive idea of identity he attributes to Cook and Careless. Bliss's 'Privatizing the Mind' (1991–92) is read only in its abstract, which does not name Cook."
