   "map_label": "'Limited identities', 1967–69",
   "evidence_note": "Cook's own text, 'Canadian Centennial Cerebrations', International Journal 22:4 (1967), pp. 659–663: the opening, read in the OpenAlex text of the article, attacks Canadian Dimension's 'Open Letter to Canadian Nationalists', likens it to Canada First's 1874 manifesto and asks 'Who says Canadian nationalism is difficult to define? Jobs!' (p. 659). The limited-identities sentence was not read on Cook's page; Buckner quotes it (2000, p. 4): 'Perhaps instead of constantly deploring our lack of identity, we should attempt to understand and explain the regional, ethnic and class identities that we do have. It might just be that it is in these limited identities that “Canadianism” is found'. Taken up: Careless (CHR 1969; unread here, quoted by Buckner, p. 4) held that the 'Canadian experience' must be 'discerned and defined' through the 'limited identities of region, culture and class referred to by Professor Cook'. Buckner (p. 5) writes that 'Cook and Careless' embraced the concept partly in response to Quebec separatism, quoting Cook's 1970 essay as reprinted in The Maple Leaf Forever (p. 213): 'Nationalism by nature tends strongly to centralism and uniformity: Canada is by nature federal, sectional and pluralist'; 'historians in Atlantic Canada eagerly espoused the concept of “limited identities”, but their motivations were rather different' (p. 5). In period, Buckner's 1988 abstract (recorded in cdng_buckner_1988, not re-read) calls it 'the catch phrase of the Canadian historical profession in the 1960s' and judges it 'premature to abandon'. Criticism: Reid (1987, opening, recorded in cdng_reid_1987) notes that during the 1980s 'concern has increasingly been expressed that fragmentation has been the result'; Gauvreau (1991, p. 164 n. 10) writes that limited identities 'has underwritten much of the “new social history”', which, like the older national history, 'marginalizes religious history'; Buckner (2000, p. 12) writes 'I think it implied to Cook and Careless' that national identity was the sum of component identities, calls that idea 'nonsensical', and argues that 'All identities are socially constructed'. Influence rather than contribution: Cook named the identities but wrote no regional history, and later questioned region as a unit of analysis (edge 2). The roster row calls him a precursor.",
