   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "The Regenerators: late Victorian social criticism read as the replacement of individual by social salvation, and the sacred becoming secular (1985)",
   "map_label": "The Regenerators, 1985",
   "evidence_note": "The book is unread here: UTP and De Gruyter refused automated access, and the Internet Archive copy is borrow-only. Its contents are recorded in crr_cook_1985, including the closing chapter 'The Sacred Becomes the Secular'. Cook's words are known as quoted in in-period readings. Gauvreau (Acadiensis 1991, pp. 161–162) quotes 'the orthodox Christian preoccupation with man's salvation was gradually replaced by a concern for social salvation' and 'the substitution of theology, the science of religion, with sociology, the science of society' (Regenerators, p. 4); Christianity became 'less rather than more relevant' (p. 6); social conservatism 'went hand in hand with scientific conservatism, and both were founded on religious conservatism' (p. 12); and social regeneration became 'a substitute for the formidable labour of reformulating theological doctrine' (p. 229). Karr (Acadiensis 1989, p. 162) quotes that the book's subjects 'almost never alluded to the cultivation of a national sentiment' (p. 6). Gaffield's review (History: Reviews of New Books 14:5, 1986, p. 144; scanned page read by OCR and checked against the image) summarizes it: 'the call for social salvation emerged from a strong religious commitment but led to increasing secularization', and Cook 'treats religious thinking as an independent factor'. Contested in period. Gauvreau (1991) calls it 'important' but says it 'does not fully resist the temptation to view the 19th-century religious experience in relation to the 20th-century mind-set' (p. 161). He reads it as a '“neo-conservative” appropriation' of Allen's categories (p. 163); 'The appearance of The Regenerators in 1985 marked the beginning of the assault on Allen's position' (p. 163 n. 9). He adds that 'the emerging consensus seems to be away from “military metaphors” of the type employed by Cook' (p. 164). Van Die, he reports, 'ultimately accepts Cook's central thesis of the decline and irrelevance of Protestant religion, but does not agree with the conflict model' (p. 173). Marshall (CCHA Historical Studies 1993–94, web text without page numbers) names 'the secularization thesis outlined by McKillop, Cook, and Marshall' as Gauvreau's target and cites Regenerators pp. 228–32 (n. 6). Laverdure's 1998 review (crr_laverdure_1998, not re-read) says Christie and Gauvreau 'challenge the secularization thesis, most strongly advocated by Ramsay Cook … and David Marshall'. Karr (1989, p. 163 n. 21) finds 'an equally forceful Ontario bias' answering Allen's Western one. The name 'secularization thesis' is the critics', as the strand note says.",
   "source_ids": [
    "crr_cook_1985",
    "pe135_gauvreau_halfway_1991",
    "cic_karr_1989",
    "pe135_gaffield_regenerators_1986",
