   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "An ecological interpretation of early Canadian history: contact as the interaction of ecologies, 'cabbages, at least as much as kings' (1990)",
   "map_label": "'Cabbages Not Kings', 1990",
   "evidence_note": "Reuses the reading recorded on 2026-10-13 for the environment-family drafts (pe131_extras_cook_1990; canon-evidence-canada.md, text C1). It was not re-read for this entry because Project MUSE refused automated access. Cook's W. L. Morton Lecture (Trent, October 1990; JCS 25:4, 1990–91) starts from Morton's 1946 remark that history 'is concerned with ecology too' (p. 5): 'It is an insight which I would like to try to build upon' (p. 6). He marks a break with the staple historians: 'for this earlier generation of historians the exploitation of the environment was accepted as natural and necessary, even though excesses might be deplored' (pp. 6–7), while 'More recent environmental historians have a different focus. They are no longer much concerned with explaining the impact of the environment on man; it is the impact of man on nature that is at the centre of their work' (p. 7). Following Crosby, 'contact was even more significantly the interaction of different ecologies or environments' (p. 13). His conclusion: 'Cabbages, at least as much as kings, led to the transformation of the new world' (p. 15, as recorded in canon-evidence-canada.md; not in the source's verification note). In-period reading: Gauvreau (Acadiensis 1991, pp. 164–165 n. 12), arguing about Cook's religious history, called the lecture his 'recent “conversion experience” concerning the happy convergence of nature and culture'. Later reading, labelled: Wynn (2004, p. 33, recorded in pe131_extras_wynn_2004) quotes Cook's claim that Canadian historical writing 'has always been concerned with the environment' to describe Canadians who 'seemed to wonder what all the fuss was about'. A contribution, not a founding. edge_913 (harold_a_innis → environment) carries his continuity claim and Forkey's 1998 rejection of it; this edge carries his own ecological reading of contact.",
   "source_ids": [
    "pe131_extras_cook_1990",
    "pe135_gauvreau_halfway_1991",
    "pe131_extras_wynn_2004"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": "environment/ecological_exchange",
   "target_strand_note": "The ecological_exchange strand (organisms, conquest and ecological exchange) is the Crosby line that the lecture applies to contact in New France (p. 13). The environment entry has no Canadian strand; the environment-family notes asked whether to add one.",
   "roster_row": null,
   "include": true
  },
  {
   "proposal_id": "pe135_cook_rel_06",
   "source": "ramsay_cook",
   "target": "who_killed_debate",
   "type": "connection",
   "relationship_kind": "contribution",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Between 'nationalists' and 'limited identitarians': class, ethnicity and gender as what Canadians share, and identities as contingent and multiple (1993–2000)",
   "map_label": "Identities contingent and multiple, 1993–2000",
   "evidence_note": "Cook's own words. 'The New History of Atlantic Canada' (Acadiensis 23:1, Autumn 1993, pp. 146–152, read in full) came between Bliss's 'Privatizing the Mind' (1991–92) and Granatstein's book (1998): 'one of the sweet ironies in the arid debate between “nationalists” and “limited identitarians” on historiography is that neither side appears to recognize that class, ethnicity and gender describe what groups of Canadians have in common, while “nationalism” most often provokes divisions — “regionalism”!' (p. 146). He holds that 'for all the emphasis on differences during recent decades, Canadians from all regions and linguistic groups have, in fact, been growing more alike, at least in aspiration if not in reality' (p. 146). His 2000 CHR forum essay (pp. 260–265 per Buckner's n. 15) is read only in its abstract: the 'limited identities' hypothesis 'has played a major role in the flowering of social history whose dominance is now being called into question by proponents of a renewed “national” approach'; the debate ignores 'the easy assumption that identities, limited or national, are essential rather than contingent', and 'it is too often forgotten that identities are multiple rather than single'. Context: Bliss's abstract (1991–92) asks historians to remember 'that their subject, after all, is Canada' and does not name Cook. Buckner (2000) writes that Cook in 1995 called the phrase 'usually attributed to Maurice Careless and I have decided that he is welcome to it!' (p. 8; Canada, Quebec and the Uses of Nationalism, 2nd ed., p. 230, unread here). Buckner then groups Bliss, Granatstein and Owram with Careless's later view that the search for limited identities had 'gone too far' (p. 9). A contribution: Cook is a root of the debate (edge 1) and a respondent who in 1993 placed himself outside both camps.",
   "source_ids": [
    "pe135_cook_atlantic_1993",
    "cdng_cook_2000",
    "cdng_bliss_1991",
    "cdng_buckner_2000"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": "who_killed_debate/reassessments_2000",
   "target_strand_note": "The reassessments_2000 strand names Cook and cites the 2000 forum; the 1993 essay is an earlier statement of the same position. limited_identities_roots carries 1967 and is covered by edge 1 at cdn_regional.",
   "roster_row": "who_killed_debate",
   "include": true
  },
