   "proposal_id": "pe135_cook_rel_07",
   "source": "ramsay_cook",
   "target": "cdn_intellectual_cultural",
   "type": "connection",
   "relationship_kind": "contribution",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Nationalism as an object of criticism: English-Canadian nationalist intellectuals read from Canada First to the 1960s, against a 'strait-jacket identity' (1967–71)",
   "map_label": "Nationalism criticised, 1967–71",
   "evidence_note": "Cook's own text, 1967 (opening read in the OpenAlex text): Canadian Dimension's call for a union of nationalists 'of all parties' is set beside the 1874 call of 'William Foster and his exuberant young friends' (Canada First), and he answers 'Who says Canadian nationalism is difficult to define? Jobs!' (International Journal 22:4, p. 659). Two passages from 1970 are known only through quotation. Karr (Acadiensis 1989, p. 162) quotes Cook (Saturday Night, April 1970): 'Every generation of Canadian nationalist intellectuals ... seems to play the part of Sisyphus. Their permanent task is to push the millstone of Canadian nationalism up a steep mountain, only to find that it rolls back down again to await the labour of the next generation. This means that much of Canadian intellectual history sounds like a broken record'. Buckner (2000, p. 4) quotes the 1970 South Atlantic Quarterly essay, reprinted in The Maple Leaf Forever (1971, pp. 197–214): Canada 'stubbornly refuses to exchange its occasionally anarchic pluralism for a strait-jacket identity. Perhaps it is this heterogeneous pluralism itself that is the Canadian identity'. In-period reading: Karr (1989, p. 162) writes that 'it was Ramsay Cook who did so much to heighten English Canada's understanding of French-Canadian nationalism who led the anti-national crusade', and that 'While it may be easy for many to agree with Cook's belittling of this recurring obsession with national identity, there are problems inherent in extremes on both sides'. His n. 18 adds: 'while Cook is opposed to divisive nationalism, he is a centralist and appears to be searching for a cohesive, positive form of national consensus' (p. 162). Gaffield (1986, p. 143) calls him 'one of Canada's major anglophone “windows” on francophone Québec'. The Maple Leaf Forever and Canada and the French-Canadian Question (1966) are unread here. The Regenerators' place in this field is carried by edge_825.",
