 "description": "A University of Toronto historian of nineteenth-century Canada and biographer of George Brown (Brown of the Globe, 1959–63). 'Frontierism, Metropolitanism, and Canadian History' (1954) criticised the Canadian historians who had adapted Turner's frontier thesis and proposed instead the study of the metropolis, a city that organizes a wide area 'through control of communications, trade, and finance'; in that view the frontier 'is in the largest sense a dependent', though 'if we reject a frontier determinism we should hardly replace it with a metropolitan determinism'. In 1969 he took up Ramsay Cook's phrase and argued that the Canadian experience must be understood through the 'limited identities of region, culture and class' (as quoted by Buckner, 2000); a decade later he added that 'Metropolitanism is not the antithesis of regionalism' and that limited identities 'should not limit his own perception' (1981). Frontier and Metropolis (1989), based on his 1987 Creighton Lectures, restated both themes. In period, Donald Davis (1985) judged his metropolitan approach 'of only limited usefulness in guiding research', and Phillip Buckner (2000) rejected the view, which he attributed to Careless and Granatstein, of regionalism as 'a construct designed to justify the dismantling of the Canadian state'.",
 "representative_figures_and_works": "J. M. S. Careless (1919–2009); “Frontierism, Metropolitanism, and Canadian History” (Canadian Historical Review, 1954); Brown of the Globe, vol. 1, The Voice of Upper Canada, 1818–1859 (1959) and vol. 2, Statesman of Confederation, 1860–1880 (1963); “‘Limited Identities’ in Canada” (Canadian Historical Review, 1969); “Limited Identities — Ten Years Later” (Manitoba History, 1981); Frontier and Metropolis: Regions, Cities, and Identities in Canada before 1914 (1989). Readings: Davis (1985); Szostak (1990); Irwin (1997); Buckner (2000).",
 "source_ids": [
