 "hunt_core_paradigm": false,
 "description": "A Toronto historian whose The Commercial Empire of the St. Lawrence (1937) built Canadian history around the one river system 'that leads from the Atlantic Seaboard to the heart of the continent', which inspired Canadians 'to build a great territorial empire, both commercial and political, in the western interior' (his own restatement of 1969); Dominion of the North (1944) calls the river 'the natural basis of a great commercial empire' and beaver fur 'the first staple of the St. Lawrence commercial system'. His two-volume John A. Macdonald (1952, 1955) completed what he called 'a trilogy on one theme'; in 1969 he accepted 'the parentage of an idea' others called 'the Laurentian interpretation of Canadian history', but rejected the story that his first book was 'the fruit of my friendship with Harold Innis'. As president of the Canadian Historical Association (1957) he attacked the Liberal 'Authorized Version' of Canadian history and the imported 'Marxian doctrine of the class struggle' and 'Frontier thesis', and in 1969 he traced the 'decline and fall' of the Empire of the St. Lawrence and of the Canada built on it, calling official bilingualism and a special position for Quebec 'a repudiation of the intentions of the Fathers of Confederation'. Contested in period: Michel Brunet (1953) found the Macdonald biography full of 'nombreux préjugés', Guy Frégault (1955) asked 'où sont les Canadiens français dans tout cela ?', and W. L. Morton, as Davis (1985) reports him, held that the metropolitan view he associated with Creighton seemed 'necessarily to minimize the importance of the hinterland'. From the late 1960s the national frame the Laurentian narrative supported gave way to regional history and 'limited identities' (Buckner, 2000).",
 "representative_figures_and_works": "Donald Creighton (1902–1979); The Commercial Empire of the St. Lawrence, 1760–1850 (1937; reissued as The Empire of the St. Lawrence, 1956); Dominion of the North: A History of Canada (1944); John A. Macdonald: The Young Politician (1952) and The Old Chieftain (1955); Presidential Address, Canadian Historical Association (1957); Harold Adams Innis: Portrait of a Scholar (1957); “The Decline and Fall of the Empire of the St. Lawrence” (1969); Canada’s First Century, 1867–1967 (1970). Readings: Heaton (1938); Brunet (1953); Frégault (1955); Woodcock (1979); Ouellet (1984); Davis (1985); Salmon (1997); Buckner (2000).",
