 {
  "proposal_id": "pe135_creighton_rel_02",
  "source": "harold_a_innis",
  "target": "donald_creighton",
  "type": "connection",
  "relationship_kind": "influence",
  "directed": true,
  "relationship": "Innis's staple studies as a resource for Creighton's Laurentian narrative, as Creighton's own staple vocabulary and later readers show; Creighton rejected the story that the 1937 book grew from their friendship",
  "map_label": "Staples behind the Laurentian narrative, contested",
  "evidence_note": "His own texts. Dominion of the North (1944) frames the St. Lawrence system by its staples ('The first staple of the St. Lawrence commercial system was beaver fur', p. 21), and its reading list (pp. 505–507) names Innis's The Fur Trade in Canada (1930) and The Cod Fisheries (1940). The 1957 presidential address recalls 'the 1930's, when the influence of Harold Innis was at its height and everybody was writing and talking about staple production' (p. 3), and exempts Innis from the Marxian fashion: 'most of the professional economists — and especially Innis — refused to accept the simplified Marxian version of historical determinism' (p. 6). In 1957 he also published Harold Adams Innis: Portrait of a Scholar (unread); Benoît Brouillette's review (1957, p. 529, read) reports Creighton's account of Innis's Canada as an east–west system 'marqué par le Saint-Laurent et les Grands-Lacs', at once 'transocéanique et transcontinental'. The qualification, his own (1969, pp. 14–15): 'The Empire of the St. Lawrence, it is sometimes confidently asserted, was the fruit of my friendship with Harold Innis ... These explanations, like most simple generalizations, happen to be wrong'; the subject was suggested 'not by Harold Innis, whom I barely knew at the time, but by W. P. M. Kennedy', and he was 'reluctantly convinced by the weight of his own evidence' about the Montreal merchants (p. 16). Readers: George Woodcock (1979, p. 134): 'Creighton's master was H. A. Innis, especially the Innis of The Fur Trade in Canada, which contained the essential germ out of which — with Innis's encouragement — Creighton built the scenario of his first book'; Fernand Ouellet (1984, p. 226): Creighton started 'partant de la théorie des staples'; Deborah Harrison (1981), as quoted by Davis (1985, p. 97): 'probably the first to advance a metropolitanist interpretation, although the closely related \"staples\" thesis had been put forward by Innis much earlier'; Salmon (1997): he 'simplified and expanded Innis' thesis'. The edge records Innis's work as a resource the Laurentian narrative used, not a teacher–pupil derivation; Creighton's denial concerns the origin of the subject, not the use of the staple framework.",
  "source_ids": [
   "pe135_creighton_dominion_1944",
   "pe135_creighton_address_1957",
   "pe135_creighton_innis_1957",
   "pe135_creighton_brouillette_1957",
   "pe135_creighton_decline_1969",
   "pe135_creighton_woodcock_1979",
   "pe135_creighton_ouellet_1984",
   "cdng_davis_1985",
   "cdng_salmon_1997",
   "cdng_innis_1930",
   "cdng_innis_1940"
  ],
  "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
  "review_status": "needs_review",
  "target_strand": null,
  "target_strand_note": "Person entries carry no strands. The matching group strand is cdn_national_tradition/laurentian, which names both men; edge_897 (harold_a_innis → cdn_national_tradition) targets the staples strand and stays.",
  "roster_row": null,
  "include": true,
  "include_note": "Kept because Creighton's own texts use the staple framework and name Innis's books, and readers from 1979 to 1997 describe the lineage. Wording follows his 1969 qualification. Reviewers may prefer to leave the lineage to the laurentian strand alone (both names already sit there); if so, set include false."
 },
 {
  "proposal_id": "pe135_creighton_rel_03",
  "source": "donald_creighton",
  "target": "progressive",
  "type": "critique",
  "relationship_kind": "critique",
  "directed": true,
  "relationship": "Turner's frontier thesis, as taken up by Canadian historians in the 1930s and 1940s, rejected as an imported 'continental revelation' that Canadian history would not fit (1957)",
  "map_label": "Against the frontier thesis, 1957",
  "evidence_note": "His own text, the 1957 CHA presidential address (read in full). Canadian historians had 'accepted, for historical purposes, the two highly fashionable political theories which dominated the thought of the inter-war and war years': 'the Marxian doctrine of the class struggle' and 'the Frontier Theory' (p. 6). Of the second: 'Turner ... fixed upon the movement of settlement across the continent as the most important fact in its history'; 'the source of creative inspiration and action was found not at the centre, but at the periphery, of western culture' (p. 8). Applied to Canada, 'painful difficulties were immediately encountered': the frontier's progress 'had been evidently modified by all sorts of extraneous and unnatural things such as railways, efficient police, governmental supervision'; in 1837 'the rebels had come, not from the frontier, but from the older settled parts of the province'; yet its believers 'kept insisting that Canadian democracy was \"forest-born\"' (p. 9). He ends: historians bought 'a couple of suits of imported intellectual reach-me-downs' and a new generation would show 'less deference for imported theories of historical change and more respect for the manifold facts of Canadian experience' (p. 12). The critique inverts his own Laurentian premise, that change radiates from the centre. Limits: the address is a polemic and names no Canadian frontierist; Turner is not quoted beyond the phrases Creighton cites, and the atlas's own reading of Turner (progressive/multiple_hypotheses) shows him advocating multiple hypotheses rather than a single doctrine. Context: Careless's 1954 CHR article, unread here, was 'conceived in part as an obituary for the frontier thesis of Frederick Jackson Turner' (Davis 1985, p. 95). No in-period reply to the address was looked for.",
  "source_ids": [
   "pe135_creighton_address_1957",
   "cdng_davis_1985",
   "cdng_careless_1954"
  ],
  "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
  "review_status": "needs_review",
  "target_strand": "progressive/progressive",
  "target_strand_note": "The progressive strand lists Turner and gives 'Turner’s frontier essay' among its works; Creighton attacks that thesis. multiple_hypotheses is Turner's later methodological address, which Creighton does not discuss.",
  "roster_row": null,
  "include": true,
  "include_note": "A critique in his own words of a position the atlas entry holds (Turner's frontier thesis). The progressive entry is a US field; edge_798 (progressive — cdn_national_tradition, comparison) stays, since it compares consensus history, not the frontier thesis."
 },
 {
