  "source": "donald_creighton",
  "target": "karl",
  "type": "critique",
  "relationship_kind": "critique",
  "directed": true,
  "relationship": "The 'Marxian doctrine of the class struggle', as Canadian party intellectuals applied it in the 1930s, rejected as alien to Canadian experience (1957)",
  "map_label": "Against the class-struggle reading of Canada, 1957",
  "evidence_note": "His own text, the 1957 presidential address (read in full): the 'Marxian economic interpretation of history' was 'a doctrine which was alien to their experience and unremunerative for their purposes' (p. 6); the League for Social Reconstruction, 'the \"brains trust\" of the Co-operative Commonwealth Federation', insisted that 'the real work of the Fathers of Confederation had been to establish an economic empire for the entrepreneurs of Montreal' (pp. 6–7); 'Canadian history was a sadly imperfect vehicle for the exemplification of the Marxian verities' (p. 7). He exempts Innis, who 'refused to accept the simplified Marxian version of historical determinism' (p. 6). The target is a Canadian application, named only through the LSR; no Marx text or Marxist historian on the karl roster is discussed.",
  "source_ids": [
   "pe135_creighton_address_1957"
  ],
  "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
  "review_status": "needs_review",
  "target_strand": null,
  "target_strand_note": "No karl strand concerns Canadian or 1930s party applications of the class-struggle doctrine.",
  "roster_row": null,
  "include": false,
  "include_note": "Excluded by the drafter: the passage criticises the LSR's use of Marxism in Canada, not a position any karl strand or roster member states, so an arrow to karl would overstate its object. Recorded for the editor; the frontier edge (3) carries the 1957 critique."
 }
]
