  "note": "Read in full for the 1.135 Creighton entry (Érudit PDF via the Internet Archive capture of 26 September 2024; UHR 14:2, October 1985, pp. 95–113). Careless's 1954 article 'conceived in part as an obituary for the frontier thesis of Frederick Jackson Turner' (p. 95); Harrison (1981, p. 25) quoted: Creighton 'probably the first to advance a metropolitanist interpretation, although the closely related \"staples\" thesis had been put forward by Innis much earlier'; Morton's 'Clio in Canada' (1980 reprint, p. 107) quoted: Creighton emphasized 'the preponderant role commercial monopoly and centralized business have played'; Morton ('Canada's History and Historians', reprint pp. 34–35) quoted: the metropolitan thesis, which he associated with Creighton and Careless, 'seems necessarily to minimize the importance of the hinterland' (pp. 97–98); Careless said he drew 'neither from Creighton or Innis' but from medieval social history and N. S. B. Gras, and melded Gras with 'the Laurentian School, then regnant' (p. 101). Morton's texts themselves are unread."
 },
