  "pe135_parr_rosenfeld_1996",
  "cdnsrc_sangster_1995",
  "cdnsrc_dubinsky_marks_1996",
  "cdnsrc_iacovetta_kealey_1996",
  "cdnsrc_sangster_1996",
  "pe135_parr_ilwch_1999",
  "cdnsrc_parr_1999",
  "pe135_parr_brush_2000",
  "pe135_parr_owram_2001",
  "pe135_parr_envhist_2006",
  "pe135_parr_sensing_2010",
  "pe135_parr_jackson_2011",
  "pe135_parr_rumiel_2011"
 ],
 "entry_kind": "person",
 "entry_type": "Individual thinker or historian",
 "scope_note": "Her own words are read in 'Nature and Hierarchy' (Atlantis, 1985, pp. 39–44, in full), the first-page extract of the 1988 CSSH article and the abstract of the 1999 ILWCH article. Her books are not read here: The Gender of Breadwinners, Domestic Goods and Sensing Changes are quoted only as their reviewers quote them, with the reviewers' page references, and the 1995 CHR essay only as Iacovetta and Kealey (1996) and Sangster (1996) quote it. In-period reviews read in full: Drystek (1980), Heron (CHR, 1991), Guildford (Atlantis, 1991), Scheinberg (Archivaria, 1991), Dennis (EcHR, 1991), Bertaux (JEH, 1992), Benson (JAH, 1993), Brush (EH.NET, 2000), Owram (BC Studies, 2001); the four Left History papers of 1995–96 are read in full. She is not presented as a founder: her 1985 essay places itself among 'a good many others' asking for the change, and the Canadian gender-history debate also turned on Pierson (1991) and Scott. The 1995 CHR essay appeared in an issue of historiographical essays, not in a CHR exchange; the exchange that took it up was in Left History. The environmental work dates from 2006–10 and is used only for the environment edge; a 2026 CHR forum on The Gender of Breadwinners is a later reading."
}

EDGE {
 "proposal_id": "pe135_parr_rel_01",
 "source": "joy_parr",
 "target": "cdn_women_gender",
 "type": "connection",
 "relationship_kind": "contribution",
 "directed": true,
 "relationship": "Gender and class as simultaneous hierarchies, and gender history as a practice in Canadian women's history (1985–96)",
 "map_label": "Gender history in practice, 1985–96",
 "evidence_note": "In-period, her own words (Atlantis, 1985, read in full): women and children are not tied together as subjects by nature; 'What distinguishes feminine and preschooler from masculine and prime of life is not naturalness versus artificiality but proximity to power' (p. 39). She criticises the mid-1970s Canadian literature on reform for characterising 1880–1920 as 'an era of \"maternal feminism\"', seeing women's public actions 'definitionally as an extension of the private' (p. 41), and asks historians to 'stop shying away from the word \"patriarchy\"' and to 'allow as a possibility that gender/sex hierarchies and class hierarchies function simultaneously, each according to its own logic, often in conflict' (p. 44). She presents this as a change 'a good many others have already begun to suggest' (p. 39). The Gender of Breadwinners (1990), unread here, as reviewers quote it: Guildford (Atlantis, 1991, p. 140) says she rejects 'an ahistorical hierarchy of oppressions' (p. 8) and argues 'that class and gender are made and experienced simultaneously'; Bertaux (JEH, 1992, p. 241) quotes p. 9 on 'these galloping pairs—class/gender, male/female, market/nonmarket, public/private' whose meanings 'are multiple and mutable'. Reception in the Left History exchange (all read in full): Sangster calls the book 'One of the path breaking books on gender history' (1995, p. 120); Iacovetta and Kealey call her 'recognized as a leading practitioner of gender history' (1996, p. 222) and 'a major figure in the field', quoting her 1995 CHR essay (pp. 362–63) that 'gender history made masculinity and femininity commensurably problematical historically, while insisting that neither could be well understood without companion inquiries into the other hierarchies with which they were mutually intertwined' (p. 228); Dubinsky and Marks cite her for refraining 'from imposing one category - be it class or gender - on historical actors' (1996, p. 211). Criticism: Sangster's reply (1996) quotes the essay's reprint (Gender and History in Canada, 1996, p. 11) as locating critics 'feminist and anti-feminist alike, in the same oppositional group' (p. 240 n. 2), notes that she had been reproached for not offering 'more criticism of historians such as Mariana Valverde and Joy Parr' (p. 240 n. 3), and, quoting Parr's line that the move to gender 'began from the post-structuralist premise that identities were made in relationships' (reprint p. 14), questions 'the prevailing assumption that gender history as the field, post-structuralism as the method, equals a superior, more broad-minded history' (pp. 244, 246). The 1995 essay itself is unread: its words here are quoted by Iacovetta and Kealey and by Sangster. Not a founding: the field's gender debate also runs through Pierson (1991) and Scott (edge_891). Later reading, labelled: a 2026 CHR 'great books' forum (abstract only) calls the book 'a path-breaking study that challenged established dichotomies in social theory'.",
 "source_ids": [
  "pe135_parr_nature_hierarchy_1985",
  "cdnsrc_parr_1990",
  "pe135_parr_guildford_1991",
  "pe135_parr_bertaux_1992",
  "cdnsrc_parr_1995",
  "pe135_parr_rosenfeld_1996",
  "cdnsrc_sangster_1995",
  "cdnsrc_iacovetta_kealey_1996",
  "cdnsrc_dubinsky_marks_1996",
  "cdnsrc_sangster_1996",
  "pe135_parr_chr_forum_2026"
 ],
 "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
 "review_status": "needs_review",
 "target_strand": "cdn_women_gender/gender_category_debate",
 "target_strand_note": "gender_category_debate holds Parr with the 1995 essay and the Left History papers, now read for this entry. Its focus says 'The replies were not read; their positions are not characterised further'; that sentence could be revised at release (this draft does not edit it). work_family_state (the 1990 book) is the alternative; postwar_domesticity is served by edge 5.",
 "roster_row": "cdn_women_gender",
 "include": true
}

EDGE {
 "proposal_id": "pe135_parr_rel_02",
 "source": "joy_parr",
 "target": "cdn_working_class",
 "type": "connection",
 "relationship_kind": "contribution",
 "directed": true,
 "relationship": "Gender in the making of class: a women's town and a men's town, Paris and Hanover, Ontario (1985–90)",
 "map_label": "The gender of breadwinners, 1990",
 "evidence_note": "In-period, her own words (Atlantis, 1985): 'students of men's work and men's participation in labour organisations', with women's historians and historians of childhood, 'have reified the skill-unskill boundary' (p. 42); 'Skill, too, is a socially constructed category' (pp. 42–43). Of the Marxist analysis in Women at Work, Ontario, 1850–1930 (1975) she writes, quoting feminist critics (n. 20: Phillips and Taylor; Hartmann), that it 'explains why a hierarchy of labour is necessary within the waged labour force, but tells us nothing about why women end up at the bottom of this hierarchy' (p. 43). The Gender of Breadwinners (1990; unread here) compares Paris and Hanover, Ontario. Heron, a labour historian, reviewing it (CHR, 1991, read in full): 'the most exhilarating, challenging book to appear in modern Canadian social history in many years'; it 'integrates and surpasses' women's, labour, technological and economic history, and 'Parr gives the boot to neo-classical analysis, simplistic Marxism, and radical feminism' (p. 218). He quotes its introduction that 'the parts and proportions of the social landscape covered by class and gender alter' (p. 10) but finds that in the 1949 Paris strike 'she has suppressed the impact of class where it was undeniably a crucial force' (p. 220). From her own figures he suggests that 'the portrait of the \"women's town\" may be slightly misleading' (p. 219). He also finds that she downplays the wars and the state and 'seldom makes any reference to the considerable Canadian literature on work, class, gender, or small-town life' (pp. 220–21). He concludes that she 'has pushed us onto new terrain where gender is neither ignored nor is it given a monocausal explanatory force' (p. 221). Scheinberg (Archivaria, 1991) sees 'a more dynamic alternative to the old and stale studies that focused more on workers' industrial and organizational activities', while doubting conclusions drawn from 'the testimony of seven women' (p. 175). Dennis (EcHR, 1991) calls it 'an important contribution to the literature of labour history and locality studies' (p. 762). A contribution from inside the field's gender strand, alongside Sangster (1989) and Bradbury (1993). It does not claim that labour history lacked gender before 1990. edge_842 and edge_889 (cdn_women_gender → cdn_working_class) already carry the entry-level contribution and critique.",
 "source_ids": [
  "pe135_parr_nature_hierarchy_1985",
  "cdnsrc_parr_1990",
  "pe135_parr_heron_1991",
  "pe135_parr_scheinberg_1991",
  "pe135_parr_dennis_1991",
  "cdnsrc_sangster_1989",
  "lof_bradbury_1993"
 ],
 "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
 "review_status": "needs_review",
 "target_strand": "cdn_working_class/gender_family_class",
 "target_strand_note": "gender_family_class ('Gender, family and class') holds Sangster, Parr and Bradbury and cites cdnsrc_parr_1990. The kind is contribution. Her 1985 criticism of how labour and women's historians treated skill is recorded here rather than as a separate critique edge: see edges_considered_not_proposed.",
 "roster_row": "cdn_working_class",
 "include": true
}

EDGE {
 "proposal_id": "pe135_parr_rel_03",
 "source": "joy_parr",
 "target": "cdn_childhood_youth",
 "type": "connection",
 "relationship_kind": "contribution",
 "directed": true,
 "relationship": "British child immigrants as labouring children (1977–80), and childhood as a history of power (1985)",
 "map_label": "Labouring children, 1980",
 "evidence_note": "In-period. Her Yale PhD (1977), 'The Home Children: British Juvenile Immigrants to Canada, 1868–1924', is recorded in the AHA Directory of History Dissertations (record 45612; adviser Robin Winks). Drystek's review of Labouring Children (Archivaria 11, 1980–81, read in full) names the thesis as the source of its sample of 997 Barnardo case histories (p. 241). He quotes the book's aims: a 'study of the life and work of child immigrants in Britain and Canada' (p. 13) that would 'reveal a darker side of evangelicalism, of turn-of-the-century childhood, and of Canadian rural life than the record has so far been allowed to express' (p. 12). His reading is critical: 'because of the authors' almost uniformly negative approach, the study is of limited value', the sample's statistical basis is unexplained, and 'the argument that a British child immigrant suffered a special burden is not convincing' (pp. 241–42). The book itself is unread here. Her own words (Atlantis, 1985) turn the subject into a question about power: she finds it troubling to treat women and children 'as part of one subject', admits to being 'part of a group who wrote, reviewed, lectured and squabbled this cluster into convention', and asks 'Is it nature which ties women and children together as subjects? Clearly not' (p. 39); the family 'is experienced by members of different generations in very different ways' (p. 44). She also edited Childhood and Family in Canadian History (1982; title-level, see edge_792). A contribution among the field's early works, not its origin: Sutherland's Children in English-Canadian Society (1976) precedes it.",
 "source_ids": [
  "pe135_parr_aha_phd",
  "cdnsrc_parr_1980",
  "pe135_parr_drystek_1980",
  "pe135_parr_nature_hierarchy_1985",
  "cdnsrc_parr_1982"
 ],
 "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
 "review_status": "needs_review",
 "target_strand": "cdn_childhood_youth/family_economy",
