 "note": "Existing people record (label Mariana Valverde; identity_note unchanged). Three roster rows exist: moral_regulation (historian), cdn_religion_reform (comparison) and liberal_order_framework (comparison). She appears in the strands moral_regulation/moral_reform, moral_regulation/governmentality_reformulations and cdn_religion_reform/secularization_comparison. On acceptance, the moral_regulation row may carry the edge_id of edge 1, the cdn_religion_reform row that of edge 2 and the liberal_order_framework row that of edge 3. She has no roster row on joan_wallach_scott, so edge 4 sets roster_row null. Open question: the moral_regulation row gives her the role 'historian'; she is a sociologist and criminologist by training (see the node). 'contributor' may be the closer role; this draft does not change the row. data/people-wikidata.json already records Q6762074 for her (method canadian_groups_1.128_2026-10-12, 'not reviewed for the atlas'); this draft reviews it (see wikidata_check)."
}
proposed_edges [
 {
  "proposal_id": "pe135_valverde_rel_01",
  "source": "mariana_valverde",
  "target": "moral_regulation",
  "type": "connection",
  "relationship_kind": "contribution",
  "directed": true,
  "relationship": "Moral regulation as the formation of ethical subjectivity, carried out by philanthropies, churches and professions as well as the state: social purity in English Canada, 1885–1925 (1988–92)",
  "map_label": "Light, soap and water: moral regulation, 1991",
  "evidence_note": "Her own in-period statements. 'Pauperism, Moral Character, and the Liberal State', Labour/Le Travail 30 (1992), pp. 213–222, read in full: 'I have argued elsewhere that the discourse of moral reform needs to be analyzed seriously, not merely dismissed as a cover for class interests', though 'moral discourse does as a matter of contingent fact legitimize certain class, gender, and racial interests' (p. 216); she credits Corrigan and Sayer's The Great Arch with pioneering 'the liberal state's role in constituting subjectivities' (p. 217). The Age of Light, Soap, and Water (1991) is known here through in-period reviewers' quotations and through Google Books search-within snippets of the 2008 University of Toronto Press reissue (volume ghqEyXf5950C; partial preview, page images not viewable here because the viewing limit was reached), which keep the 1991 pagination: reformers sought 'to reshape the ethical subjectivity of both immigrants and native-born Canadians' (p. 17; quoted by Dehli 1992, p. 316); purity work 'was not simply a question of banning obscene books or suppressing prostitution but was rather a campaign to educate the next generation' (p. 17; quoted by McCormick 1992, p. 124); the reform movements 'helped to shape the bourgeoisie, which led the movements, as well as the working class' (p. 15; Dehli, p. 318); 'Moral regulation is very closely linked to state formation, as the British school of historical sociology has argued; but as Foucault and his followers have pointed out, the state has no monopoly on moral regulation' (p. 165; snippet, the last clause also quoted by Dehli, p. 317); '[m]oral regulation is an important aspect of ruling, helping to constitute class, gender, sexual, and race relations' (p. 166, as quoted by Wright 1995, p. 124). Her notes, seen only as snippets, place the concept: in The Great Arch 'the role of the state in moral regulation is highlighted, and their theorization has been influential here; but the agencies of regulation internal to civil society are obscured' (n. 34, p. 171); Corrigan and Sayer 'do not differentiate between moral and other modes of social regulation; by contrast, I restrict the term to mean the formation of ethical subjectivity' (p. 173). Note 33 (p. 171) cites the 1988 article with Lorna Weir, which is unread here. In-period reception by historians. Tina Loo (Labour/Le Travail 30, 1992, pp. 277–279, read) calls the book 'a provocative and valuable contribution to the history of social reform and, more significantly, because of its self-conscious use of theory, to the writing of Canadian history' (p. 279). Donald Wright (Acadiensis, 1995, pp. 122–126 read) calls it a 'much talked about treatise' by 'a sociologist' (p. 122) and its concept of 'slippages' among class, gender, sexuality and race her 'most original' contribution (p. 124). Carolyn Strange and Tina Loo's Making Good: Law and Moral Regulation in Canada (1997; Google Books snippets, pp. 5, 63) write that 'Theorists influenced by philosopher Michel Foucault have urged scholars to abandon the social-control model for a more flexible concept of regulation' (p. 5) and cite 'Mariana Valverde and others' on prostitution as 'the social evil' (p. 63). Mary Louise Adams's 'In Sickness and in Health: State Formation, Moral Regulation, and Early VD Initiatives in Ontario' (1994) is cited at title level only. In-period criticism. Loo finds the second argument, on philanthropy and the state, 'more asserted than proved' (p. 279). Kari Dehli (Historical Studies in Education, 1992, pp. 315–319, read) would draw 'not quite so sharply drawn' conclusions about state and civil society and finds that she moves 'quickly from analysis of discursive strategies ... to drawing conclusions about people's motives and intentions' (pp. 318–319). Wright judges her claim that audiences 'knew precisely what was meant' 'untenable' and finds 'the impoverished immigrant and the \"fallen woman\" ... conspicuously silent', so that 'the silence of the consumer amidst the din of the producer gives the impression of top-down social control, precisely what Valverde sought to avoid' (pp. 125–126). Chris McCormick (Atlantis, 1992, pp. 123–125, read) asks how her interpretations are made and judges that a 'thorough' reading of context is 'optimistic' (p. 123), while agreeing that 'Regulation is a better word than control' (p. 124). Sociologists reformulated the concept: Alan Hunt's 1997 abstract (read) treats moral regulation not as 'a specific moral arena' but as 'a component of all fields of social relations', a wider definition than hers; in Governing Morals (1999; snippets) he writes 'I do not take issue with Valverde's invocation of ethical subjectivity, but I emphasise the mundane features of self-governing' (p. 155). Dean (1994) and Ruonavaara (1997) are cited through their abstracts as on the moral_regulation node. A contribution, not a founding: Corrigan (1981) and Corrigan and Sayer (1985) precede her, and she credits them. Diseases of the Will (1998), on alcohol and the governance of the will, is title-level here (snippets only) and does not support this edge.",
  "source_ids": [
   "lof_valverde_1991",
   "pe135_valverde_pauperism_1992",
   "cdnsrc_valverde_weir_1988",
   "cdnsrc_corrigan_sayer_1985",
   "pe135_loo_review_1992",
   "pe135_dehli_review_1992",
   "pe135_mccormick_review_1992",
   "cpl129_wright_1995",
   "cdnsrc_strange_loo_1997",
   "cdnsrc_adams_1994",
   "cdnsrc_hunt_1997",
   "cdnsrc_hunt_1999",
   "cdnsrc_dean_1994",
   "cdnsrc_ruonavaara_1997"
  ],
  "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
  "review_status": "needs_review",
  "target_strand": "moral_regulation/moral_reform",
  "target_strand_note": "moral_reform ('Moral reform and social purity'; 'reform as a productive, not only repressive, project') holds her, Weir, Strange and Loo, and lof_valverde_1991; it is exactly the checked claim. governmentality_reformulations also holds her, for Diseases of the Will (1998), which is only snippet-level here.",
  "roster_row": "moral_regulation",
