 },
 {
  "proposal_id": "pe135_valverde_rel_03",
  "source": "mariana_valverde",
  "target": "liberal_order_framework",
  "type": "connection",
  "relationship_kind": "comparison",
  "directed": false,
  "relationship": "Liberalism read as a system of moral regulation and the liberal state as a constitutor of subjectivities (1991–92), beside the liberal order framework's later history of property, the individual and rule",
  "map_label": "Liberalism as moral regulation, 1992",
  "evidence_note": "Her own in-period statements, before McKay's prospectus (2000). 'Pauperism, Moral Character, and the Liberal State', Labour/Le Travail 30 (1992), pp. 213–222, read in full, endorses Mitchell Dean's argument 'that classical English liberalism was not a negation of state regulation but rather a particular system of economic, social, and moral regulation', so that 'a history of pauperism is also a genealogy of liberalism' (p. 217). She reviews Jean-Marie Fecteau's Un nouvel ordre des choses (1989) as 'a landmark work' whose 'theoretical sophistication' crosses 'the traditional historiographical boundaries separating coercion from benevolence and the state as a whole from charity' (pp. 221–222), but faults its 'moyens de contrôle' as 'a rather functionalist formulation', a 'model of negative class power', and adds that 'These moral codes are not intrinsic to the logic of capital, since capitalism works quite efficiently in different cultural systems' (p. 222). Note 10 (p. 220) cites Fecteau's essay in Greer and Radforth's Colonial Leviathan (1992), and the essay thanks Allan Greer for comments on a draft (p. 222). In the 1991 book (Google Books search-within snippets of the 2008 reissue, 1991 pagination) note 35 (p. 171) cites Bruce Curtis's 'Preconditions of the Canadian State', and she writes that moral regulation 'might be an important social process, which is intertwined with but is not an effect of either the economic or the political' (p. 165) and that 'one has to be sceptical of any claims that envision a clear separation between moral and economic regulation' (p. 166). Reviewers read the book's state argument in the same terms: private philanthropy 'allowed liberal democratic states to adhere to their policy of non-interference in the private sphere' (Loo 1992, p. 278, her paraphrase). Comparison only. The user decided (2026-10-12) that the liberal order framework is 'a different project closer to history of capitalism'; Fecteau and Curtis are shared ground (state formation), not membership. No in-period text by the framework's authors discussing her was read here (McKay's 2000 prospectus is paywalled and unread). edge_848 (liberal_order_framework – moral_regulation) is the field-level comparison; this edge records her own 1992 statement of the boundary.",
  "source_ids": [
   "pe135_valverde_pauperism_1992",
   "lof_valverde_1991",
   "pe135_loo_review_1992",
   "lof_greer_radforth_1992",
   "lof_mckay_2000"
  ],
  "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
  "review_status": "needs_review",
  "target_strand": "liberal_order_framework/state_formation_roots",
  "target_strand_note": "state_formation_roots ('State formation before the framework': Curtis, Greer, Radforth) is the ground her 1991 notes and 1992 essay share with the framework (Curtis; Colonial Leviathan). Fecteau sits in the property_and_capitalism and critiques strands for his 2009 reply, which is later than the 1989 book she reviewed.",
  "roster_row": "liberal_order_framework",
