source_addenda {
 "lof_valverde_1991": {
  "status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked",
  "checked_on": "2026-10-14",
  "note": "For the 1.135 person entry. The book's pages were not read. Wording is known from (a) in-period reviewers' quotations, read: Dehli 1992 (pp. 15, 17, 165), McCormick 1992 (pp. 9, 17, 42, 43, 90), Loo 1992 (pp. 35, 36, 41, 164), Wright 1995 (pp. 9–13, 41, 42, 43, 89–90, 98, 103, 166); and (b) Google Books search-within snippets of the 2008 UTP reissue (volume ghqEyXf5950C), whose page images were not viewable. The reissue keeps the 1991 pagination for the original text: the snippets put the reviewers' pp. 15, 17, 165 and 166 on the same pages, and the 1991 preface's p. 9 at p. ix (so 1991 p. 10 = 2008 p. x). Crossref chapter records: Preface ix–xiv; Introduction to the 2008 Edition 1–14; 1. Introduction 15–33; Notes 168–201. Snippet wording used: 'Following but modifying Foucault's approach, I take the term \"discourses\" to refer not to language as separate from the real world but rather to organized sets of signifying practices' (p. x); 'Moral regulation is very closely linked to state formation, as the British school of historical sociology has argued; but as Foucault and his followers have pointed out, the state has no monopoly on moral regulation' (p. 165); moral regulation 'might be an important social process, which is intertwined with but is not an effect of either the economic or the political' (p. 165); 'Marxist accounts (and in Canada this chiefly means accounts produced by the political economy school)' (p. 165); 'one has to be sceptical of any claims that envision a clear separation between moral and economic regulation' (p. 166); n. 33 (p. 171) cites Valverde and Weir 1988; n. 34 (p. 171): in The Great Arch 'the role of the state in moral regulation is highlighted, and their theorization has been influential here; but the agencies of regulation internal to civil society are obscured'; n. 35 (p. 171) cites Bruce Curtis, 'Preconditions of the Canadian State'; p. 173: Corrigan and Sayer 'do not differentiate between moral and other modes of social regulation; by contrast, I restrict the term to mean the formation of ethical subjectivity'. The words before 'the term \"moral regulation\" from' on p. 173 were not visible. Acknowledgements (p. viii, snippet): 'Lorna Weir has been my best critic'."
 },
 "pl129_valverde_1990": {
  "status": "supporting_page_checked",
  "checked_on": "2026-10-14",
  "note": "Re-read in full for the 1.135 person entry (lltjournal.ca article 4766, galley 5639). Page assignments from the running heads: 'the controversy about discourse theory' (p. 227); Scott's approach 'has much to commend it', her four elements 'somewhat arbitrary', 'transcending the idealism/materialism dichotomy' (p. 230); limitations 'symptomatic of the general limitations of discourse analysis as it has been used by social historians (myself included!)' (p. 231); 'an inherent bias in discourse analysis in favour of what some call \"the sociology of domination.\" This problem, not discussed by Scott'; 'rather polemical book tends to sing the praises of discourse analysis without mentioning many of its problems'; 'the grandiose claim made by Scott on Foucault's behalf' (p. 232); 'she exaggerates the contribution Foucault and Derrida can make to historical work'; 'deserves to be carefully read even by those who find some of its more polemical claims hard to swallow' (p. 233)."
 },
 "cpl129_wright_1995": {
  "status": "supporting_page_checked",
  "checked_on": "2026-10-14",
  "note": "Valverde section re-read for the 1.135 person entry, pp. 122–126 (UNB PDF 12013/12857). Wright reviews The Age of Light, Soap, and Water as one of six books. Passages used: 'In her much talked about treatise'; the six authors include 'one a sociologist (Mariana Valverde)'; 'Following Foucault' (p. 122); 'Valverde is quick to avoid a \"descent into discourse\"'; she 'modifies Foucault's archaeology' and 'maintains an important commitment to social agency'; quotes the book's p. 43 on 'the decentring of the subject' (p. 123); quotes p. 166, '[m]oral regulation is an important aspect of ruling, helping to constitute class, gender, sexual, and race relations'; 'the concept of slippages', 'her most exciting, most original, contribution' (p. 124); her conclusion that audiences 'knew precisely what was meant' is 'untenable'; 'the impoverished immigrant and the \"fallen woman\" remain conspicuously silent' (p. 125); 'the silence of the consumer amidst the din of the producer gives the impression of top-down social control, precisely what Valverde sought to avoid' (p. 126)."
 },
 "crr_christie_gauvreau_1996": {
  "status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked",
  "checked_on": "2026-10-14",
  "note": "For the 1.135 person entry: Google Books search-within snippets (volume zcfujZowlDwC, partial preview; page images not viewed). Wording recorded but not page-image checked: 'historians such as Doug Owram and Mariana Valverde have adhered to a caricature of the antiquated and esoteric Victorian theologue to excise the churches from \"progressive\" currents of modern thought and social action' (p. xiii); 'Valverde has argued that in early twentieth-century Canada issues such as social purity and immigration were controlled by a hegemonic exercise of power by the state and middle-class organizations' (p. 187); 'Valverde's analysis wrongly posits the view that given the hegemonic influence of the state, there was only one option regarding immigration policy – that of racism and the annihilation of immigrant culture'; 'three divergent strands of opinion on the immigration question' (p. 188); n. 95 (p. 328) cites Valverde pp. 15, 112, 123–8 (and gives the subtitle's end date as 1914). Note 5 to p. xiii (p. 252) also discusses her book; its inner quotation ('are superseded by concerns about good citizenship and the imposition of bureaucratic and state control') was not found in her book by search and is not attributed to her."
 },
 "cdnsrc_strange_loo_1997": {
  "status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked",
  "checked_on": "2026-10-14",
  "note": "For the 1.135 person entry: Google Books search-within snippets (volume lnKrT6fay9AC; page images not viewed). 'Theorists influenced by philosopher Michel Foucault have urged scholars to abandon the social-control model for a more flexible concept of regulation. Many scholars have accordingly tossed away the image of the hammer in favour of that of the net – restrictive, yet full of holes. Regulation theorists have also emphasized that state agencies and institutions are not the sole means by which behaviour is regulated' (p. 5); 'As Mariana Valverde and others have argued, Canadians, like their British and U.S. counterparts, inflated prostitution into the master problem of the age – \"the social evil.\"' (p. 63). The further-reading list (p. 158) gives the 1991 book."
 },
 "cdnsrc_hunt_1999": {
  "status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked",
  "checked_on": "2026-10-14",
  "note": "For the 1.135 person entry: Google Books search-within snippets (volume FD59ivQ8KxQC; page images not viewed). 'Valverde has argued that projects of moral regulation are not so much concerned with changing behaviour as with the ...' and 'I do not take issue with Valverde's invocation of ethical subjectivity, but I emphasise the mundane features of self-governing' (p. 155); 'Valverde is correct to identify moral regulation as being focused on the production of \"ethical subjectivities\" (Valverde 1991: 216)' (p. 189). Hunt's page reference 216 exceeds the 1991 book's 205 pages; unresolved."
 },
 "cdnsrc_valverde_1998": {
  "status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked",
  "checked_on": "2026-10-14",
  "note": "For the 1.135 person entry: Google Books search-within snippets (volume Kl5ugmvDgH0C; page images not viewed). The introduction places liquor control where 'moral regulation, fiscal policy, and administrative law were mixed and managed with very little public input' (p. 9) and criticises studies that 'artificially isolate a single mode of governance, assuming that what is to be studied is medicalization, or professionalization, or the shift from disciplinary control to risk-based management' (p. 10; the words before were not visible). Not used as evidence for any edge."
 },
 "cdnsrc_valverde_weir_1988": {
  "status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked",
  "checked_on": "2026-10-14",
  "note": "Still unread. Valverde's own 1991 note 33 (p. 171; Google Books snippet of the 2008 reissue) gives the subtitle as 'More Preliminary Remarks on Moral Regulation' (RFR 17, 3, September 1988, pp. 31–34); Weir's York profile gives 'Preliminary Remarks'. The 'More' would echo Corrigan's 1981 'Some Preliminary Remarks'. Not resolved; the title is left as it stands."
 }
}

