pe135_berger_monet_1972 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full (Érudit PDF, text layer; header gives 'University of Toronto Press, 1970. 277 p.'). Quoted: imperialists 'ont eu très mauvaise presse parmi les historiens du Canada'; English-Canadian historians 'partisans pour la plupart de l'explication de l'équipe gagnante' saw them as 'des conservateurs retardataires'; Berger 'vient maintenant nuancer ces jugements... téméraires' (p. 274); the imperialists were not 'des agents d'une puissance étrangère' but 'de vrais Canadians nationalistes' (pp. 274–275); the thesis, 'déjà suggérée par la monographie de Norman Penlington', 'sera dorénavant très difficile, sinon impossible, à réfuter'; earlier criticisms: economic and military neglect, pan-Britannic witnesses, 'une petite élite' (p. 275; notes cite R. J. D. Page, JCS Aug. 1970, 39–43; D. L. Cole, JCS, 44–49; H. V. Nelles, Canadian Forum April–May 1970, 10–12; the OCR misplaces the note numbers but keeps their order); 'une contribution majeure — sinon un premier modèle — à l'histoire des idées (oui, il y en a) au Canada anglophone' and French Canadians' 'toute petite' place in imperialist thought (p. 276). Cites the book at pp. 132 and 261, which confirms that the 2013 reprint keeps the 1970 pagination."}
pe135_berger_mills_1977 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full (journal PDF on the UVic OJS archive; issue table of contents gives Allen Mills, pp. 134–137; issue published 30 April 1977). Quoted: The Sense of Power saw the imperialists' ideas 'as essentially a species of Canadian nationalism'; the new book 'makes a substantial contribution to the intellectual history of English Canada'; the 'superluminaries' and 'the book is really about them' (p. 135); Berger's 'main criticism of English-Canadian historians' (pp. 135–136) with Berger's 1976 pp. 259–260 quoted; 'places much too much weight on that over-used term' and 'very different conceptions of the nation that is to survive' (p. 136); the claim about class, region, culture and family 'is unimpeachable' (pp. 136–137); the last chapter 'all too brief' (p. 137)."}
pe135_berger_tcook_1977 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full (Archivaria PDF; citation metadata pp. 198–203; running foot 'Archivaria, Number 5, 1977-78'). Quoted: Berger's 'pioneering work' in 'intellectual history' dating to 1970 (p. 199); 'a remarkably subtle analysis of major trends in Canadian historiography'; it 'transcends historiography to become an intellectual history of English Canada in the twentieth century'; a 'mirror' of 'the major contours in Canadian nationalist thought'; 'recipient of the Governor-General's Award for Non-Fiction' (p. 202); 'limited identities' as 'the wave of the future' and the nation-building passage cited to Berger p. 263 (p. 203). Cook does not say the passage quotes Careless; the 1986 edition (p. 262, snippet) does. OCR errors in the PDF corrected silently."}
pe135_berger_cole_1970 {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Article unread (closed). Crossref metadata (JCS 5:3, Aug. 1970, pp. 44–49). Its argument is known only as others report it: Monet (1972, p. 275, n. 3) — witnesses 'qui seraient plutôt des représentants du pan-britannisme'; Karr (1989, pp. 162–163, n. 20) — 'over-Canadianization of imperialism'; Owram (2013, p. xxvii, snippet; later reading) — imperialism as 'a Britannic movement'. Published beside Robert J. D. Page, 'Carl Berger and the Intellectual Origins of Canadian Imperialist Thought, 1867–1914', JCS 5:3 (1970): 39–43 (Crossref metadata only)."}
pe135_berger_cole_1971 {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Body closed. Read on Cambridge Core: the first-page extract (historians 'employed in the same nation-making industry'; 'difficulties of terminology, conceptualization, and ideological inflection') and the reference list. n. 6 cites Berger's 1966 Toronto thesis 'The Vision of Grandeur' (pp. 1, 11) and The Sense of Power (1970) with Penlington (1965); n. 31 calls Berger's 'The True North Strong and Free' (1966) 'excellent'; n. 33: 'Berger, A Sense of Power, makes the strongest case for a Canadian nationalistic consciousness within these terms.' The article's own argument is not read."}
pe135_berger_carr_1982 {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Article unread (closed). Crossref metadata (JCS 17:2, May 1982, pp. 91–99; title as registered, 'Revisionalist') and the journal abstract it carries: 'revisionists such as Carl Berger, while ostensibly challenging Whig assumptions about imperialism/nationalism, actually share many of the traditional views'; 'their handling of terminology is inept and their context, both historical and historiographical, inadequate'; 'symptomatic of a general parochialism in the writing of Canadian history'. The abstract is in the third person and may be the journal's. Karr (1989, n. 20) cites it as 'Revisionist', vol. XVIII, 2 (Summer 1982), p. 91, and quotes Carr's 'scavenger hunt for nationalism' (p. 163)."}
pe135_berger_owram_2013 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Later reading. Crossref chapter record (pp. xi–xxx; title page: 'With a New Introduction by Doug Owram'; signed 'University of British Columbia, January 2013'). Google Books snippet view only: the thesis made 'pretty much the standard interpretation of the way in which Canadian nationalism evolved' (p. xi); on Cole, imperialism 'was a Britannic movement', answered 'While a reasonable point, the lack of comparative perspective in no way undermined the primary thesis of the book' (p. xxvii); cites Bell (CJPS 1972, p. 322), Preston (AHR 1972), Page and Cole (pp. xxv–xxvii). Not evidence for any edge."}

