pe135_cook_burden_1977 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in the UNB Libraries Acadiensis PDF (OJS article 11456; the OJS record misspells the author 'Ramsey Cook'; the review is signed RAMSAY COOK, p. 115; running heads give pp. 111–115, and the first page is 110 per the OJS record). Volume and issue follow Buckner 2000 n. 15 (VII, 1); Buckner dates it Autumn 1974, but the OJS record and the 1977 books reviewed, Bercuson 1977 and Alexander's Canadian Forum letter of September 1977 (n. 1), date it 1977. Quoted: 'the tendency to see regionalism as a virtue of Canadian life rather than an obstacle to Canadian unity'; the Laurentian view hardly acceptable 'as “national”' in the Fraser, Red or Restigouche valleys, 'a point made long ago by W. L. Morton'; 'And the other nationalist themes fare little better' (p. 110); Bercuson's contributors assume 'that Canadian history is best understood from a regional perspective' (p. 112); no one 'attempts to define the term' (p. 114); the regional approach 'has both strengths and weaknesses. The whole may not be greater than the parts, but it is at least the sum of them' (p. 115)."}
pe135_cook_unmasked_1983 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in the UNB Libraries Acadiensis PDF (OJS article 12212; OJS author 'Ramsey Cook'; signed RAMSAY COOK, p. 142; running heads pp. 137–142). Volume and issue follow Buckner 2000 n. 15 (XIII, 1, Autumn 1983). The first words of p. 137 ('SINCE THE 19ÔOS') are garbled in the text layer, so the decade is not quoted. Quoted: the 'national' approach 'has come under a destructive barrage of criticism'; Berger's book 'a lengthy epitaph on the headstone of the national school's grave'; Morton's 'Clio in Canada' 'made explicit what had long been implicit in the Innis-Creighton laurentian thesis' (p. 137); Wynn's chapter 'the finest section of the book' (p. 138); 'I have begun to doubt seriously the existence of the phenomena we have for so long called regions'; 'So why not call a region what it really is — a province' (p. 140); 'as a tool of analysis, “regionalism” is a concept whose time has gone'; 'Stripped of its rhetoric ... a legitimate preoccupation with ... class, sex, family, ethnicity' (p. 141). Buckner 2000 (p. 8) quotes the key sentence as 'whose time is gone'; the page reads 'has gone'."}
pe135_cook_atlantic_1993 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in the UNB Libraries Acadiensis PDF (OJS article 11967). The citation line printed on p. 146 gives Acadiensis XXIII, 1 (Autumn, 1993), pp. 146–152; signed RAMSAY COOK. Quoted: 'one of the sweet ironies in the arid debate between “nationalists” and “limited identitarians” on historiography is that neither side appears to recognize that class, ethnicity and gender describe what groups of Canadians have in common, while “nationalism” most often provokes divisions — “regionalism”!'; Canadians 'have, in fact, been growing more alike, at least in aspiration if not in reality' (p. 146); Histoire du Québec contemporain makes Quebec 'what Michel Brunet always denied: a “normal” society' (p. 147); the Atlantic Provinces as 'the scene of fragmentation and conflict' (p. 152). His note on being introduced as a Torontonian who subscribed to Acadiensis 'from the beginning' (p. 147) is the passage Buckner 2000 (p. 8) cites."}
pe135_gauvreau_halfway_1991 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "UNB Libraries Acadiensis PDF (OJS article 11891), read pp. 158–166 and 173–174 with notes; page numbers from the running heads. Volume, issue and pages agree with Marshall 1993–94 n. 2 and Kiesekamp (Acadiensis 1995) n. 47. Quoted: The Regenerators 'does not fully resist the temptation to view the 19th-century religious experience in relation to the 20th-century mind-set'; Christianity 'less rather than more relevant' (Regenerators p. 6) (p. 161); Cook's words from Regenerators pp. 4, 12 and 229 (p. 162); a '“neo-conservative” appropriation' of Allen's categories (p. 163); n. 9, 'The appearance of The Regenerators in 1985 marked the beginning of the assault on Allen's position' (p. 163); 'military metaphors' of the type employed by Cook (p. 164); n. 10, limited identities 'has underwritten much of the “new social history”' and 'marginalizes religious history' (p. 164); n. 12, Cook's 'recent “conversion experience”' in 'Cabbages Not Kings' (pp. 164–165); Van Die 'ultimately accepts Cook's central thesis' but not his conflict model (p. 173); Fraser against 'Richard Allen and Ramsay Cook' (p. 174)."}
pe135_gaffield_regenerators_1986 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in the University of Ottawa repository scan (uO Research, hdl 10393/36463; DOI 10.20381/ruor-20743), a two-page image PDF with no text layer, transcribed by OCR. The repository citation gives vol. 14, no. 5 (1986): 143–4; the first scan page carries the printed folio 143 and a handwritten note 'v. 14, No. 5, May/June 1986, pp 143-4'. Quoted: Cook as 'professor of history at York University' and 'one of Canada's major anglophone “windows” on francophone Québec' (p. 143); 'the call for social salvation emerged from a strong religious commitment but led to increasing secularization'; Cook 'treats religious thinking as an independent factor' (p. 144). It also notes that 'readers may wonder about how Cook chose his characters, sources, and quotes' and that the book is limited to Protestant English Canada (p. 144). The quotations were checked against the page images rendered at 200 dpi."}
pe135_cook_portnoy_1970 {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Metadata only: OpenAlex W4361854129 and the Duke University Press DOI landing text (closed access; PDF only). The text is unread. Its wording appears in the atlas only as quoted by Buckner 2000 (p. 4, and p. 5 quoting the reprint at p. 213), and Karr 1989 cites it beside a Saturday Night piece of April 1970 (p. 162 n. 17). The reprint pages follow Buckner's n. 3."}
pe135_cook_french_canadian_1966 {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Metadata only: Internet Archive catalogue records canadafrenchcan00cook and canadafrenchcana00cook (Toronto: Macmillan of Canada, 1966; borrow-only); OpenAlex lists a 1966 International Journal review under the same title. Karr 1989 (p. 162 n. 11) cites it, with the title slipped to 'French Canada and The Canadian Question'. Unread."}

 careless
pe135_careless_manitoba_1981 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full on 2026-10-14 in the Manitoba Historical Society's online text (no page numbers; quotations are unpaginated). The editor's note says the lecture inaugurated the James A. Jackson memorial lectures and reflected on ten years since the 1969 article; the text refers to the 1978 Cook bicentennial as 'last year' and to 'the close of the 1970s', so it was given in 1979. The society's page dates the issue 1981; Buckner (2000, p. 9, n. 16) cites it as Manitoba History 1:1 (1980), p. 7. Passages used: the 1969 summary ('had tended to bulk larger ...') and the quotation of its p. 10; the farmer 'in the midst of a flood'; 'I scarcely caused the flood'; n. 1, 'I did not invent the term “limited identity”, but acquired it from an article by Ramsay Cook ... essentially a commentary on his initial idea'; the quotation from the 1973 working-class bibliography; urban history and 'the interplay of city and region'; 'Metropolitanism is not the antithesis of regionalism. Each is inherent in the other'; 'diversity Unlimited'; 'limited identities remain crucial subjects for the historian's regard — but they should not limit his own perception'; 'cut the sight-lines through'. n. 8 credits Cook (1977) with anticipating 'the next stage for “limited identities”'."}
pe135_careless_szostak_1990 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full on 2026-10-14 in the open PDF (journal pages 499–501) from the University of Alberta repository. Passages used: the book comprises the three 1987 Creighton Lectures and a fourth lecture given at the University of Victoria, and 'The key word in this volume, as it has been throughout his work, is metropolitanism' (p. 499); metropolitanism 'neither naturally benign nor naturally exploitive'; frontier expansion 'quite capital-intensive and therefore strongly directed by metropolitan forces'; 'Cities become the foci of regional identity'; Atlantic Canada's identity 'despite having no one dominant metropolis'; on the final chapter, 'the concept of metropolitanism provides little additional insight' (p. 108 of the book); 'Others are likely to find themselves left unconvinced'; 'Economic historians especially may object to the descriptive rather than analytical nature of the work' (all p. 500)."}
pe135_careless_irwin_1997 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Abstract only, read on 2026-10-14 through OpenAlex (which cites both Careless articles among the works the article cites); the article is paywalled and unread. Passages used: prairie historians 'working within the conceptual framework of the Metropolitan thesis or Limited Identities' locate prairie identity in protest against the external metropolis; identity 'must be understood as an autonomous layer of consciousness rather than a “limited identity” within a national consciousness'."}
pe135_careless_brown_globe {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Metadata only. Checked against Open Library work OL3544781W and editions OL13982909M (vol. 1, 1959, 354 pp., 'Vol.1, The voice of Upper Canada 1818-1859') and OL13982910M (vol. 2, 1963, 406 pp., 'Vol.2, Statesman of Confederation 1860-1880'); Internet Archive holds lending copies only. The biography is unread; no review of it was read."}

 creighton
pe135_creighton_decline_1969 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full (pp. 14–25) from page images of the Érudit PDF, via the Internet Archive capture of 25 July 2024 (the live Érudit site returned a bot-check page); OCR checked against the page order and running heads. Quoted: the phrase 'made its first public appearance in the autumn of 1937' (he names the book The Empire of the St. Lawrence, its 1956 title); regarded 'as the inspiration, at least in part, of a new school of Canadian historians and as the genesis of a new historical doctrine, the Laurentian interpretation of Canadian history'; 'no wish to disavow the parentage of an idea'; the book as 'the fruit of my friendship with Harold Innis' called a simple generalization that 'happen[s] to be wrong' (p. 14); subject suggested 'not by Harold Innis, whom I barely knew at the time, but by W. P. M. Kennedy'; the Chateau Clique merchants and 'Montreal's staple trades' (p. 15); 'reluctantly convinced by the weight of his own evidence'; 'The protagonist of the Empire of the St. Lawrence was the River itself'; Macdonald as 'the only satisfactory method of writing the second phase' and 'a trilogy on one theme'; 'This Laurentian theme ...' and 'a great territorial empire, both commercial and political, in the western interior' (p. 16); 'the east-west axis would be its backbone' (p. 17); Confederation 'not of a cultural compact between two cultural groups' (p. 18); the First World War as 'the great divide' (p. 19); Mackenzie King 'not so much a Canadian citizen as a citizen of North America' (p. 21); 'Canada now stands alone' and 'American economic and military imperialism' (p. 22); 'ethnic and cultural values' (p. 24); duality, bilingualism and a special position for Quebec 'a repudiation of the intentions of the Fathers of Confederation' (p. 25)."}
pe135_creighton_address_1957 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full (pp. 1–12) from page images of the Érudit PDF, via the Internet Archive capture of 20 November 2022; OCR checked against running heads. Quoted: 'In the 1930's, when the influence of Harold Innis was at its height and everybody was writing and talking about staple production' (p. 3); 'the Liberal Interpretation — or Authorized Version — of Canadian history' begun by Willison and Dafoe (p. 4); historians 'accepted, for historical purposes, the two highly fashionable political theories', 'the Marxian doctrine of the class struggle' and 'the Frontier Theory' (p. 6); the Marxian interpretation 'alien to their experience and unremunerative for their purposes'; Innis 'refused to accept the simplified Marxian version of historical determinism' (p. 6); the LSR claim about 'an economic empire for the entrepreneurs of Montreal' (pp. 6–7); 'a sadly imperfect vehicle for the exemplification of the Marxian verities' (p. 7); Turner 'fixed upon the movement of settlement across the continent as the most important fact in its history' (p. 8); frontier progress 'modified by ... railways, efficient police, governmental supervision', the 1837 rebels 'not from the frontier', 'forest-born' (p. 9); 'How wrong we were!' (p. 11); 'imported intellectual reach-me-downs' and 'less deference for imported theories of historical change' (p. 12)."}
pe135_creighton_dominion_1944 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Open Internet Archive copy (dominionofnorthh0000dona; Houghton Mifflin, Riverside Press, copyright 1944). Read: title and copyright pages, contents (pp. v–vii), pp. 3–6, 21, 24–25, 166, 185, 270, 272, 298, 419 and the reading list pp. 505–507; the rest unread. Page numbers from the printed running heads (the OCR's leaf-to-page map is offset). Open Library edition OL6465350M (LCCN 44003898). Quoted: 'the one great river system which led to the Atlantic seaboard from the heart of the continent', 'the natural basis of a great commercial empire stretching far into the distant west', 'The first staple of the St. Lawrence commercial system was beaver fur' (p. 21); the struggle 'between the northern and southern routes, between Montreal and New York' (pp. 24–25); the Grand Trunk 'reinforced the old commercial empire of the St. Lawrence' (p. 270); 'Quebec had been the origin of the empire of the St. Lawrence; it was to be the genesis of the Dominion of Canada' (p. 298); Montreal 'the capital of the fur-trading empire of the St. Lawrence' and 'the triumph of the east-west route' (p. 419); the reading list includes Innis's The Fur Trade in Canada (1930) and The Cod Fisheries (1940) (p. 506) and his own British North America at Confederation (p. 507)."}
pe135_creighton_innis_1957 {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Book unread. Open Library edition OL213681M (University of Toronto Press, 1957, 146 p., LCCN 58000854); reissued 1978 (OL4287173M). Content known only through Brouillette's review (pe135_creighton_brouillette_1957)."}
pe135_creighton_first_century_1970 {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Book unread (Internet Archive copies are borrow-only). Open Library editions OL4062517M (Macmillan of Canada, 1970, 372 p.) and OL5219565M (St. Martin's Press, 1970). Its argument is not characterised from the book; the node's account of his later nationalism rests on the 1969 lecture."}
pe135_creighton_heaton_1938 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "First page only (p. 565), read in the Cambridge Core first-page image (article S0315489000026591); the rest is closed. Crossref: CJEPS 4:4, November 1938, pp. 565–570, author Heaton. Review header: Ryerson Press and Yale University Press, 1937, pp. xx, 441, in the Carnegie Endowment's Relations of Canada and the United States series. Quoted: since Lilian Knowles 'I have often wondered who would pick up her mantle as exponent of the economic interpretation of history ... Now I think my search has ended'; the book quoted as a 'study in commerce and politics' and an 'attempt to trace the relations between the commercial system of the St. Lawrence and the political development of Canada during almost a century of its history' (Heaton's quotation of the book; not checked against the book); 'he certainly has applied the test with uncanny and relentless skill, and has pleased neither the Marxians nor their enemies'."}
pe135_creighton_brunet_1953 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full (Érudit PDF, Internet Archive capture of 28 March 2023). Quoted: Creighton 'l'un des maîtres contemporains de l'historiographie anglo-canadienne' (p. 579); the biographer's weakness of presenting 'son héros comme le centre-moteur du développement historique'; 'Il n'a pas jugé nécessaire de dépouiller un seul journal canadien-français' (p. 580); 'Il est évident que M. Creighton entretient de nombreux préjugés'; he seems to want to demolish 'tous ceux qui s'opposent, de nos jours, à la centralisation fédérale' (p. 581); the book succeeds only 'à demi' (p. 582). Brunet quotes Creighton's vol. 1, pp. vii and 442–443."}
pe135_creighton_fregault_1955 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full (Érudit PDF, Internet Archive capture of 11 August 2022). Quoted: the work 'fondé sur une compréhension exacte de l'histoire du Canada'; 'un homme ne crée pas une nation. Seule, une société peut y parvenir' (p. 444); 'Nationalisme antiaméricain, impérialisme et centralisation : synthèse canadienne, — je veux dire anglo-canadienne' (p. 446); 'Car où sont les Canadiens français dans tout cela ? Tout simplement, ils n'y sont pas' (p. 447). A mixed review: it praises the book's coherence and calls it worth reading for French Canadians (p. 448)."}
pe135_creighton_brouillette_1957 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full (Érudit PDF, Internet Archive capture of 21 December 2025). Brouillette reports the biography's account of Innis's Canada: 'L'axe économique du Canada est, au contraire, le système orienté est-ouest, concurrent de son voisin, marqué par le Saint-Laurent et les Grands-Lacs ... Cet axe vital est à la fois transocéanique et transcontinental' (p. 529). It is the reviewer's summary of Creighton's portrait, not Innis's own words."}
pe135_creighton_woodcock_1979 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full (journal PDF on UBC's OJS; issue 83, pp. 131–141). A literary critic's essay, not a historian's. Quoted: Creighton 'glorifying the transcontinental conception of the original Dominion-builders', and his Maclean's article 'No More Concessions' (27 June 1977) (p. 133); 'Creighton's master was H. A. Innis ... with Innis's encouragement' (p. 134); the Macdonald volumes 'the first important study of the most interesting political personality Canada has yet produced' (p. 135); 'the dominant vision is spatial even more than temporal' (p. 137); Creighton's 1972 introduction to Towards the Discovery of Canada calling the Liberal view 'almost a complete inversion of my own' (p. 139; quoted by Woodcock, not read); 'a major distortion in Creighton's view of Canadian history' and 'the fallacy that the Fathers of Confederation did not intend to create a genuine federalism' (p. 140)."}
pe135_creighton_ouellet_1984 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Pp. 225–229 read (Érudit PDF, Internet Archive capture of 15 August 2022, text layer); the rest unread. Quoted: Creighton, 'partant de la théorie des staples pour établir les bases économiques du développement de la nation canadienne', held that New France's economy rested on the fur trade and was led by a bourgeoisie (p. 226, n. 13 citing the 1937 and 1944 books); 'le chef de l'Ecole laurentienne'; his post-Conquest French Canada 'une société semi-féodale décadente devenue hostile aux impératifs économiques'; Ouellet asks whether the later scheme of Séguin, Frégault and Brunet is 'bien différent' (p. 227)."}

 parr
pe135_parr_nature_hierarchy_1985 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in the journal's PDF (article/download/4974/4172); citation metadata from the article page (vol. 11, no. 1, 1985, pp. 39–44). Pages assigned by PDF order (p. 42 is printed). Passages used: 'Is it nature which ties women and children together as subjects? Clearly not'; 'proximity to power'; 'a good many others have already begun to suggest' (p. 39); 'an era of \"maternal feminism\"' (p. 41); 'students of men's work and men's participation in labour organisations, have reified the skill-unskill boundary' (p. 42); 'Skill, too, is a socially constructed category' (pp. 42–43); Women at Work (1975) and the Marxist analysis that 'tells us nothing about why women end up at the bottom of this hierarchy', quoted from feminist critics (n. 20: Phillips and Taylor; Hartmann) (p. 43); 'stop shying away from the word \"patriarchy\"'; 'gender/sex hierarchies and class hierarchies function simultaneously, each according to its own logic, often in conflict' (p. 44)."}
pe135_parr_cssh_1988 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Crossref DOI record (CSSH 30:3, 1988, pp. 511–533). Only the first-page extract and the first three notes on the Cambridge Core article page were read; the body is closed access. Extract: 'economists and feminist theorists have been interested in sexual divisions as general features of the economic or sex/gender system rather than as boundaries between tasks forged in defined contexts by particular clashes of interest'; 'most analysts have dealt with gender division as a characteristic of the work force as a whole'."}
pe135_parr_heron_1991 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full: four page scans (pp. 218–21, CHR running heads) hosted on the author's site, OCR'd with tesseract and read against the page order. Signed 'CRAIG HERON York University'. Volume and pages from the page heads and the site's listing ('Vol. 72, 1991, pp.218-21'); issue not checked. Passages: 'the most exhilarating, challenging book to appear in modern Canadian social history in many years'; 'integrates and surpasses'; 'Parr gives the boot to neo-classical analysis, simplistic Marxism, and radical feminism' (p. 218); 'the portrait of the 'women's town' may be slightly misleading', from the book's own figures (p. 219); the book's p. 10, 'the parts and proportions of the social landscape covered by class and gender alter', and 'she has suppressed the impact of class where it was undeniably a crucial force' (p. 220); wars and the state 'particularly lightly dealt with'; 'seldom makes any reference to the considerable Canadian literature on work, class, gender, or small-town life'; 'new terrain where gender is neither ignored nor is it given a monocausal explanatory force' (p. 221)."}
pe135_parr_guildford_1991 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in the journal PDF (article/download/4229/3473/5635); author, volume, issue and pages 140–141 from the article page metadata (dated 1991-10-01). Passages: she rejects 'an ahistorical hierarchy of oppressions' (book p. 8) and argues 'that class and gender are made and experienced simultaneously'; 'the relationships among industry, domesticity, and community' (book p. 6); the book 'has changed the agenda and the terms of debate in Canadian history'; 'Parr's style is dense and sometimes cryptic'."}
pe135_parr_scheinberg_1991 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in the journal PDF (article/download/11776/12726); running head 'ARCHIVARIA 32', pp. 173–175. Passages: the oral testimony of 'seven women' behind the conclusion about men's domestic work; 'a more dynamic alternative to the old and stale studies that focused more on workers' industrial and organizational activities' (p. 175)."}
pe135_parr_dennis_1991 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full: page scans (pp. 761–62) hosted on the author's site (GB-EcHistRsmscreen.pdf), OCR'd; Crossref DOI record (EcHR 44:4, 1991, from p. 761). Passages: 'goes far beyond previous accounts of connections between capitalism and patriarchy' (p. 761); graphs that 'cavalierly use straight lines to connect a handful of isolated observations'; 'an important contribution to the literature of labour history and locality studies' (p. 762)."}
pe135_parr_bertaux_1992 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full: page scans (pp. 239–41) hosted on the author's site (GB-JEHsmscreen.pdf), OCR'd; the p. 9 quotation was checked against the page image ('market/nonmarket'). Crossref DOI record. Passages: the book's p. 9, 'the ways in which social identities are simultaneously formed from a multiplicity of elements' and 'that the meanings of these galloping pairs—class/gender, male/female, market/nonmarket, public/private—behind which our thinking has trailed, are moot; that these meanings . . . are multiple and mutable' (p. 241); wish for 'a basic quantitative summary of work force data' (p. 240); 'not strictly a \"women's issue\"' (p. 241)."}
pe135_parr_benson_1993 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full: one-page scan hosted on the author's site, OCR'd; Crossref DOI record (JAH 79:4, 1993, from p. 1630); page break checked on the image. Passages: 'few historians have successfully integrated the discussion of these two fundamental relationships'; 'Parr joins this small but growing group' (p. 1630); 'Like others influenced by post-structuralist theory, she emphasizes the shifting and multivalent meanings often obscured by dualisms'; 'gives gender and class coordinate roles in her analysis but downplays ethnicity' (p. 1631)."}
pe135_parr_rosenfeld_1996 {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Open Library work OL3020950W (Joy Parr; first published 1996; publisher Copp Clark Pitman; ISBN 077305541X). Rosenfeld's co-editorship is from Parr's site and Sangster (1996, n. 2), which cites Parr's 'Gender History and Historical Practice' in this volume at pp. 11, 14 and 21. Contents unread."}
pe135_parr_drystek_1980 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in the journal PDF (article/download/10856/11773); author and pages 240–242 from the article page metadata; the article page shows Archivaria 11 (Winter 1980–81). Passages: the book's aims (pp. 12–13); the Barnardo sample of 997 children 'originally compiled for Parr's Ph.D. thesis, \"The Home Children: British Juvenile Immigrants to Canada, 1868-1924\" (Yale, 1977)' (p. 241); 'almost uniformly negative approach'; 'the argument that a British child immigrant suffered a special burden is not convincing' (p. 242)."}
pe135_parr_aha_phd {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Record 45612 in the atlas's harvest (data/field-research/aha-dissertations-2026-10-13/records.csv): dissertator 'Parr, Joy'; Yale Univ., Dept. of History; completed 1977; adviser Robin Winks. Confirmed by Drystek's review (Archivaria 11, p. 241: same title and dates, 'Yale, 1977') and by Queen's University's 2024 notice ('PhD from Yale University in 1977'). The thesis is unread."}
pe135_parr_ilwch_1999 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Crossref DOI record (ILWCH 55, 1999, pp. 112–128). Abstract read on the Cambridge Core article page; the issue theme there is 'Class and Consumption'. Body closed. Passages: 'How much consumption matters is a cultural difference, a shared national predicament, a politics grounded in the circumstances of class, and learned as a gendered responsibility'; 'When producers were not paramount in the regulation of consumption, to what priorities was commerce subsidiary?'"}
pe135_parr_brush_2000 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in the copy archived by MITH's Women's Studies reading room ('Published by EH.NET (February, 2000)'; 'Reviewed for H-Business and EH.NET by Pippa Brush'). Quotations of the book with its pages: 'an archeology of the material, moral, and economic choices and constraints which formed Canadian commodity culture in the two decades after the Second World War' (p. 17); questions 'How much can citizens talk back to manufacturers and the state about domestic goods?' (pp. 3–4); 'naturalize[d] these hortatory American norms as the intrinsic qualities of \"consumer society\"' (p. 11); 'puts studies of material culture into unaccustomed company' (p. 3); three sections, the third 'on household technology' (p. 10); 'reasoned and resisting hope' (p. 270)."}
pe135_parr_owram_2001 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in the journal PDF (article/download/185199/184562/192100); issue 129 and date 2001 from the article page metadata; pages 103–105 from the running heads (OCR'd as 'IOJ', 'I04', 'zo$'). Passages: 'the book's strength is in its parts rather than in some seamless thesis' (p. 103); 'Parr argues, the assumption of consumer sovereignty is facile' (p. 104); 'Canadians, both men and women, preferred Fords to Volvos, and that is what they got' (p. 105)."}
pe135_parr_envhist_2006 {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Crossref DOI record (Environmental History 11:2, 2006, pp. 269–299). Her site gives pp. 282–312. Text unread."}
pe135_parr_sensing_2010 {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Crossref DOI record (UBC Press, 2010; ISBN 9780774817257; chapter records give a Foreword pp. xi–xxii and an Introduction pp. 1–24); Open Library OL16309299W (2010). The De Gruyter chapter PDFs sat behind a bot wall; the book is unread and is quoted only as Jackson (2011) quotes it."}
pe135_parr_jackson_2011 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in the PDF hosted on the author's site; Crossref DOI 10.2752/174589311X12961584846007 (vol. 6, no. 2, pp. 225–228). Passages: the book's p. 9, 'representations . . . in language . . . , to open interpretive space in which to study the robust materiality of technologies, environments, and the everyday, to encounter them as directly and as fleshly as possible'; chapter headings 'Smell and Risk', 'Taste and Expertise'; proprioception, kinesthetics and proxemics; Nature/History/Culture series, general editor Graeme Wynn (p. 225)."}
pe135_parr_rumiel_2011 {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in an Internet Archive capture (10 February 2020) of the journal PDF (lh.journals.yorku.ca/index.php/lh/article/download/35836/32554); the live site is behind a bot check. Pages 188–89 from the running heads. Crossref gives a placeholder date (1969); the issue is 2010–11 ('final draft' stamp 2/3/11). Passages: 'the people whose lives and senses of self had been transformed by them'; a comparative Aboriginal or Inuit case as a missed opportunity (p. 189)."}
