cdn_women_gender {'id': 'gender_category_debate', 'title': "Gender history and women's history: the dichotomies exchange", 'focus': 'Whether gender history extends or displaces women’s history. Pierson’s 1991 essay opens on the turn to deconstruction and Scott. Sangster’s “Beyond Dichotomies” (1995), by her own later account, protested “the hierarchy being established between a superior gender history and a ‘limited’ women’s history”; replies by Dubinsky and Marks and by Iacovetta and Kealey defended gender history against her dichotomy, and she answered (1996). She later found the exchange “not very satisfying, with discussants sometimes talking at cross-purposes”. The replies were not read; their positions are not characterised further.', 'person_ids': ['ruth_roach_pierson', 'joy_parr', 'joan_sangster', 'franca_iacovetta', 'linda_kealey', 'karen_dubinsky'], 'works': 'Ruth Roach Pierson, "Experience, Difference, Dominance and Voice in the Writing of Canadian Women\'s History," in Writing Women\'s History, ed. Karen Offen, Ruth Roach Pierson and Jane Rendall (Basingstoke: Macmillan, 1991), 79–106.; Joy Parr, "Gender History and Historical Practice," Canadian Historical Review 76, no. 3 (1995): 354–376.; Joan Sangster, "Beyond Dichotomies: Re-Assessing Gender History and Women\'s History in Canada," Left History 3, no. 1 (Spring/Summer 1995): 109–21.; Karen Dubinsky and Lynne Marks, "Beyond Purity: A Response to Sangster," Left History 3, no. 2, and 4, no. 1 (Fall 1996): 205–20.; Franca Iacovetta and Linda Kealey, "Women\'s History, Gender History and Debating Dichotomies," Left History 3, no. 2, and 4, no. 1 (Fall 1996): 221–37.; Joan Sangster, "Reconsidering Dichotomies," Left History 3, no. 2, and 4, no. 1 (Fall 1996): 239–48.; Joan Sangster, Through Feminist Eyes (2011), introduction.', 'source_ids': ['cdnsrc_pierson_1991', 'cdnsrc_parr_1995', 'cdnsrc_sangster_1995', 'cdnsrc_dubinsky_marks_1996', 'cdnsrc_iacovetta_kealey_1996', 'cdnsrc_sangster_1996', 'cdnsrc_sangster_2011'], 'basis': 'editorial_distinction'}
