pe135_creighton_decline_1969 Read in full (pp. 14–25) from page images of the Érudit PDF, via the Internet Archive capture of 25 July 2024 (the live Érudit site returned a bot-check page); OCR checked against the page order and running heads. Quoted: the phrase 'made its first public appearance in the autumn of 1937' (he names the book The Empire of the St. Lawrence, its 1956 title); regarded 'as the inspiration, at least in part, of a new school of Canadian historians and as the genesis of a new historical doctrine, the Laurentian interpretation of Canadian history'; 'no wish to disavow the parentage of an idea'; the book as 'the fruit of my friendship with Harold Innis' called a simple generalization that 'happen[s] to be wrong' (p. 14); subject suggested 'not by Harold Innis, whom I barely knew at the time, but by W. P. M. Kennedy'; the Chateau Clique merchants and 'Montreal's staple trades' (p. 15); 'reluctantly convinced by the weight of his own evidence'; 'The protagonist of the Empire of the St. Lawrence was the River itself'; Macdonald as 'the only satisfactory method of writing the second phase' and 'a trilogy on one theme'; 'This Laurentian theme ...' and 'a great territorial empire, both commercial and political, in the western interior' (p. 16); 'the east-west axis would be its backbone' (p. 17); Confederation 'not of a cultural compact between two cultural groups' (p. 18); the First World War as 'the great divide' (p. 19); Mackenzie King 'not so much a Canadian citizen as a citizen of North America' (p. 21); 'Canada now stands alone' and 'American economic and military imperialism' (p. 22); 'ethnic and cultural values' (p. 24); duality, bilingualism and a special position for Quebec 'a repudiation of the intentions of the Fathers of Confederation' (p. 25).
pe135_creighton_address_1957 Read in full (pp. 1–12) from page images of the Érudit PDF, via the Internet Archive capture of 20 November 2022; OCR checked against running heads. Quoted: 'In the 1930's, when the influence of Harold Innis was at its height and everybody was writing and talking about staple production' (p. 3); 'the Liberal Interpretation — or Authorized Version — of Canadian history' begun by Willison and Dafoe (p. 4); historians 'accepted, for historical purposes, the two highly fashionable political theories', 'the Marxian doctrine of the class struggle' and 'the Frontier Theory' (p. 6); the Marxian interpretation 'alien to their experience and unremunerative for their purposes'; Innis 'refused to accept the simplified Marxian version of historical determinism' (p. 6); the LSR claim about 'an economic empire for the entrepreneurs of Montreal' (pp. 6–7); 'a sadly imperfect vehicle for the exemplification of the Marxian verities' (p. 7); Turner 'fixed upon the movement of settlement across the continent as the most important fact in its history' (p. 8); frontier progress 'modified by ... railways, efficient police, governmental supervision', the 1837 rebels 'not from the frontier', 'forest-born' (p. 9); 'How wrong we were!' (p. 11); 'imported intellectual reach-me-downs' and 'less deference for imported theories of historical change' (p. 12).
pe135_creighton_dominion_1944 Open Internet Archive copy (dominionofnorthh0000dona; Houghton Mifflin, Riverside Press, copyright 1944). Read: title and copyright pages, contents (pp. v–vii), pp. 3–6, 21, 24–25, 166, 185, 270, 272, 298, 419 and the reading list pp. 505–507; the rest unread. Page numbers from the printed running heads (the OCR's leaf-to-page map is offset). Open Library edition OL6465350M (LCCN 44003898). Quoted: 'the one great river system which led to the Atlantic seaboard from the heart of the continent', 'the natural basis of a great commercial empire stretching far into the distant west', 'The first staple of the St. Lawrence commercial system was beaver fur' (p. 21); the struggle 'between the northern and southern routes, between Montreal and New York' (pp. 24–25); the Grand Trunk 'reinforced the old commercial empire of the St. Lawrence' (p. 270); 'Quebec had been the origin of the empire of the St. Lawrence; it was to be the genesis of the Dominion of Canada' (p. 298); Montreal 'the capital of the fur-trading empire of the St. Lawrence' and 'the triumph of the east-west route' (p. 419); the reading list includes Innis's The Fur Trade in Canada (1930) and The Cod Fisheries (1940) (p. 506) and his own British North America at Confederation (p. 507).
pe135_creighton_innis_1957 Book unread. Open Library edition OL213681M (University of Toronto Press, 1957, 146 p., LCCN 58000854); reissued 1978 (OL4287173M). Content known only through Brouillette's review (pe135_creighton_brouillette_1957).
pe135_creighton_first_century_1970 Book unread (Internet Archive copies are borrow-only). Open Library editions OL4062517M (Macmillan of Canada, 1970, 372 p.) and OL5219565M (St. Martin's Press, 1970). Its argument is not characterised from the book; the node's account of his later nationalism rests on the 1969 lecture.
pe135_creighton_heaton_1938 First page only (p. 565), read in the Cambridge Core first-page image (article S0315489000026591); the rest is closed. Crossref: CJEPS 4:4, November 1938, pp. 565–570, author Heaton. Review header: Ryerson Press and Yale University Press, 1937, pp. xx, 441, in the Carnegie Endowment's Relations of Canada and the United States series. Quoted: since Lilian Knowles 'I have often wondered who would pick up her mantle as exponent of the economic interpretation of history ... Now I think my search has ended'; the book quoted as a 'study in commerce and politics' and an 'attempt to trace the relations between the commercial system of the St. Lawrence and the political development of Canada during almost a century of its history' (Heaton's quotation of the book; not checked against the book); 'he certainly has applied the test with uncanny and relentless skill, and has pleased neither the Marxians nor their enemies'.
pe135_creighton_brunet_1953 Read in full (Érudit PDF, Internet Archive capture of 28 March 2023). Quoted: Creighton 'l'un des maîtres contemporains de l'historiographie anglo-canadienne' (p. 579); the biographer's weakness of presenting 'son héros comme le centre-moteur du développement historique'; 'Il n'a pas jugé nécessaire de dépouiller un seul journal canadien-français' (p. 580); 'Il est évident que M. Creighton entretient de nombreux préjugés'; he seems to want to demolish 'tous ceux qui s'opposent, de nos jours, à la centralisation fédérale' (p. 581); the book succeeds only 'à demi' (p. 582). Brunet quotes Creighton's vol. 1, pp. vii and 442–443.
pe135_creighton_fregault_1955 Read in full (Érudit PDF, Internet Archive capture of 11 August 2022). Quoted: the work 'fondé sur une compréhension exacte de l'histoire du Canada'; 'un homme ne crée pas une nation. Seule, une société peut y parvenir' (p. 444); 'Nationalisme antiaméricain, impérialisme et centralisation : synthèse canadienne, — je veux dire anglo-canadienne' (p. 446); 'Car où sont les Canadiens français dans tout cela ? Tout simplement, ils n'y sont pas' (p. 447). A mixed review: it praises the book's coherence and calls it worth reading for French Canadians (p. 448).
pe135_creighton_brouillette_1957 Read in full (Érudit PDF, Internet Archive capture of 21 December 2025). Brouillette reports the biography's account of Innis's Canada: 'L'axe économique du Canada est, au contraire, le système orienté est-ouest, concurrent de son voisin, marqué par le Saint-Laurent et les Grands-Lacs ... Cet axe vital est à la fois transocéanique et transcontinental' (p. 529). It is the reviewer's summary of Creighton's portrait, not Innis's own words.
pe135_creighton_woodcock_1979 Read in full (journal PDF on UBC's OJS; issue 83, pp. 131–141). A literary critic's essay, not a historian's. Quoted: Creighton 'glorifying the transcontinental conception of the original Dominion-builders', and his Maclean's article 'No More Concessions' (27 June 1977) (p. 133); 'Creighton's master was H. A. Innis ... with Innis's encouragement' (p. 134); the Macdonald volumes 'the first important study of the most interesting political personality Canada has yet produced' (p. 135); 'the dominant vision is spatial even more than temporal' (p. 137); Creighton's 1972 introduction to Towards the Discovery of Canada calling the Liberal view 'almost a complete inversion of my own' (p. 139; quoted by Woodcock, not read); 'a major distortion in Creighton's view of Canadian history' and 'the fallacy that the Fathers of Confederation did not intend to create a genuine federalism' (p. 140).
pe135_creighton_ouellet_1984 Pp. 225–229 read (Érudit PDF, Internet Archive capture of 15 August 2022, text layer); the rest unread. Quoted: Creighton, 'partant de la théorie des staples pour établir les bases économiques du développement de la nation canadienne', held that New France's economy rested on the fur trade and was led by a bourgeoisie (p. 226, n. 13 citing the 1937 and 1944 books); 'le chef de l'Ecole laurentienne'; his post-Conquest French Canada 'une société semi-féodale décadente devenue hostile aux impératifs économiques'; Ouellet asks whether the later scheme of Séguin, Frégault and Brunet is 'bien différent' (p. 227).

