CAREFUL NODE A University of Toronto historian of nineteenth-century Canada and biographer of George Brown (Brown of the Globe, 1959–63). 'Frontierism, Metropolitanism, and Canadian History' (1954) criticised the Canadian historians who had adapted Turner's frontier thesis and proposed instead the study of the metropolis, a city that organizes a wide area 'through control of communications, trade, and finance'; in that view the frontier 'is in the largest sense a dependent', though 'if we reject a frontier determinism we should hardly replace it with a metropolitan determinism'. In 1969 he took up Ramsay Cook's phrase and argued that the Canadian experience must be understood through the 'limited identities of region, culture and class' (as quoted by Buckner, 2000); a decade later he added that 'Metropolitanism is not the antithesis of regionalism' and that limited identities 'should not limit his own perception' (1981). Frontier and Metropolis (1989), based on his 1987 Creighton Lectures, restated both themes. In period, Donald Davis (1985) judged his metropolitan approach 'of only limited usefulness in guiding research', and Phillip Buckner (2000) rejected the view, which he attributed to Careless and Granatstein, of regionalism as 'a construct designed to justify the dismantling of the Canadian state'.
RUDIN ADDENDUM {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Berger passages checked 2026-10-14 in Google Books snippet view (volume Bh5dDMnmFdQC; fragments only). p. 3: Berger's book 'dealt with the situation in English Canada'; p. 4: 'I share Berger's belief that historical writing can “reveal a good deal about the intellectual climate in which it was composed”' and 'I also fundamentally differ with him on several counts. For instance, I do not accept his assumption, implicit in Writing of Canadian History, that historians across the twentieth century have been united by their search for the truth'; p. 6: Berger's view 'is at odds with the one that will be developed in this book'; p. 55: Berger's conclusion that French-Canadian writing was 'remote from some of the chief developments in English-Canadian historiography in the inter-war years'; p. 58: 'Berger failed, however, to consider the way in which Groulx had, by the 1930s, altered his view of the Dollard affair'."}
cdnsrc_sangster_1995 {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Addendum 2026-10-14 (Claude, Parr draft): read in full in an Internet Archive capture (4 September 2024) of the journal PDF (lh.journals.yorku.ca/index.php/lh/article/download/5312/4507); the live site is behind a bot check. Pages 109–21 by running head. On p. 120 she calls The Gender of Breadwinners 'One of the path breaking books on gender history', written 'by Joy Parr, an established historian', in arguing that there is no 'definite age split' between women's and gender historians. She cites Palmer's 'Canadian Controversies' (History Today, 1994) on gender history's 'parochialism' (p. 120 n. 34)."}
cdnsrc_dubinsky_marks_1996 {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Addendum 2026-10-14 (Claude, Parr draft): read in full in an Internet Archive capture (15 February 2020) of the journal PDF (article/download/6967/6151). Pages 205–20 by running head. They answer Sangster's dichotomies, citing Parr's Gender of Breadwinners (p. 209 n. 6). 'As Joy Parr has recently shown, an understanding of identity requires a willingness to refrain from imposing one category - be it class or gender - on historical actors' (p. 211, n. 11: the 1990 book and the 1995 CHR essay). They defend attention to age, marital status and religion as well as gender, race and class (pp. 210–11)."}
cdnsrc_iacovetta_kealey_1996 {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': 'Addendum 2026-10-14 (Claude, Parr draft): read in full in an Internet Archive capture (9 February 2020) of the journal PDF (article/download/6968/6152). Pages 221–37 by running head. \'Joy Parr, recognized as a leading practitioner of gender history\' (p. 222). \'a major figure in the field, Joy Parr, says that "gender history made masculinity and femininity commensurably problematical historically, while insisting that neither could be well understood without companion inquiries into the other hierarchies with which they were mutually intertwined"\' (p. 228, n. 13: CHR 1995, pp. 362–63). Gender history\'s stress on discourse, \'discourses are the medium through which experiences are comprehensible\' (p. 229, n. 15: Parr 1995, p. 365). \'one of the key problems with the gender history approach - how to understand the "whys" and the "whos" rather than the "hows"\' (p. 229). They propose treating gender history \'as an approach/epistemology rather than as a field in direct competition with women\'s history\' (p. 228).'}
cdnsrc_sangster_1996 {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Addendum 2026-10-14 (Claude, Parr draft): read in full in an Internet Archive capture (15 February 2020) of the journal PDF (article/download/6970/6154). Pages 239–48 by running head. In n. 2 (p. 240) she quotes Parr's 'Gender History and Historical Practice' (in Parr and Rosenfeld, eds., Gender and History in Canada, 1996, p. 11) and says it tends 'to locate critics of gender history, feminist and anti-feminist alike, in the same oppositional group'. In n. 3 (p. 240) she notes she was reproached for not offering 'more criticism of historians such as Mariana Valverde and Joy Parr'. On p. 244 she quotes Parr (p. 14) that the move to gender 'began from the post-structuralist premise that identities were made in relationships', adding that the two 'have blossomed in tandem'. She concedes she had 'congealed and possibly blurred two issues' (p. 244). She questions 'the prevailing assumption that gender history as the field, post-structuralism as the method, equals a superior, more broad-minded history' (p. 246; n. 14 cites Parr p. 21)."}

