data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/valverde/entry-draft.json:206:    "new": "Drawn as a contribution, the atlas's usual kind for Foucault's outgoing edges. Valverde's own acknowledgement (checked 2026-10-14 for revision 1.135, in Google Books search-within snippets of the 2008 reissue, 1991 pagination): 'Following but modifying Foucault's approach, I take the term \"discourses\" to refer not to language as separate from the real world but rather to organized sets of signifying practices' (preface, p. x) and 'as Foucault and his followers have pointed out, the state has no monopoly on moral regulation' (p. 165; also quoted by Dehli 1992, p. 317). Wright (Acadiensis, 1995, p. 123, read) reads her as modifying 'Foucault's archaeology' with 'an important commitment to social agency'. Strange and Loo (1997, p. 5, snippet): 'Theorists influenced by philosopher Michel Foucault have urged scholars to abandon the social-control model for a more flexible concept of regulation'."
