data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/valverde/entry-draft.json:214:    "evidence_note": "Valverde studies the moral fervour of Protestant social reformers (publisher description); the religion-and-reform entry studies the same churches and social gospel as religious history (Christie and Gauvreau 1996, title-level). The two fields read the reformers differently; no critique is asserted because the checked texts do not state one. PhD context: 67 of 164 2009–2026 PhDs engaging Moral regulation also engage Religion, social reform & secularization in Canada (41%; lift 3.97), counted after removing shared anchors. Bibliographies record consultation, not influence; lifts are high for most Canadian pairs. Valverde places moral reform at the centre of English Canada's race, class and gender order; Cook's Regenerators covers the same reformers' religious thought. PhD context: 25 of 42 PhDs citing The Regenerators also cite Valverde (lift 7.2). Comparison only.",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/valverde/entry-draft.json:222:    "evidence_note": "Valverde studies the moral fervour of Protestant social reformers (publisher description); the religion-and-reform entry studies the same churches and social gospel as religious history (Christie and Gauvreau 1996, title-level). The two fields read the reformers differently. In period, Loo (Labour/Le Travail 30, 1992, p. 278, read) asked Valverde's book to 'address Christianity and certainly Protestantism more fully, even if only to dismiss its importance', and Christie and Gauvreau (1996, p. xiii, Google Books snippet) said she and Doug Owram used 'a caricature of the antiquated and esoteric Victorian theologue to excise the churches'. Those objections concern one book and are recorded on the edge from the mariana_valverde entry (revision 1.135); this field-level edge stays a comparison. PhD context: 67 of 164 2009–2026 PhDs engaging Moral regulation also engage Religion, social reform & secularization in Canada (41%; lift 3.97), counted after removing shared anchors. Bibliographies record consultation, not influence; lifts are high for most Canadian pairs. Valverde places moral reform at the centre of English Canada's race, class and gender order; Cook's Regenerators covers the same reformers' religious thought. PhD context: 25 of 42 PhDs citing The Regenerators also cite Valverde (lift 7.2). Comparison only.",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/valverde/entry-draft.json:233:    "new": "The two fields read the reformers differently. In period, Loo (Labour/Le Travail 30, 1992, p. 278, read) asked Valverde's book to 'address Christianity and certainly Protestantism more fully, even if only to dismiss its importance', and Christie and Gauvreau (1996, p. xiii, Google Books snippet) said she and Doug Owram used 'a caricature of the antiquated and esoteric Victorian theologue to excise the churches'. Those objections concern one book and are recorded on the edge from the mariana_valverde entry (revision 1.135); this field-level edge stays a comparison."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/valverde/entry-draft.json:246:    "note": "Read in full (pp. 213–222) in the journal's own PDF (lltjournal.ca article 4860, galley 5733; citation line on p. 213: Labour/Le Travail, 30 (Fall 1992), 213-22). Passages used: 'a critique of political economy for its failure to theorize moral regulation and subjectivity generally' (pp. 213–214); 'moral capital of the poor' (p. 215); 'the discourse of moral reform needs to be analyzed seriously, not merely dismissed as a cover for class interests' and 'moral discourse does as a matter of contingent fact legitimize certain class, gender, and racial interests' (p. 216); Dean shows 'that classical English liberalism was not a negation of state regulation but rather a particular system of economic, social, and moral regulation'; 'a history of pauperism is also a genealogy of liberalism'; Dean 'undercuts the Marxist view that moral regulation is an unimportant offshoot of economic class relations by expanding the analysis of the liberal state's role in constituting subjectivities pioneered by Corrigan and Sayer' (p. 217); Fecteau's study 'sadly lacking' Dean's awareness of gender (p. 220); n. 10 on Fecteau's essay in Colonial Leviathan and his perspective 'not to my knowledge otherwise represented in Canadian historiography' (p. 220); 'moyens de contrôle', 'a rather functionalist formulation', 'a model of negative class power', 'the pitfalls of marxist functionalism', 'These moral codes are not intrinsic to the logic of capital', 'a landmark work' (p. 222); thanks to Lynne Marks and Allan Greer (p. 222)."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/valverde/entry-draft.json:346:   "note": "For the 1.135 person entry. The book's pages were not read. Wording is known from (a) in-period reviewers' quotations, read: Dehli 1992 (pp. 15, 17, 165), McCormick 1992 (pp. 9, 17, 42, 43, 90), Loo 1992 (pp. 35, 36, 41, 164), Wright 1995 (pp. 9–13, 41, 42, 43, 89–90, 98, 103, 166); and (b) Google Books search-within snippets of the 2008 UTP reissue (volume ghqEyXf5950C), whose page images were not viewable. The reissue keeps the 1991 pagination for the original text: the snippets put the reviewers' pp. 15, 17, 165 and 166 on the same pages, and the 1991 preface's p. 9 at p. ix (so 1991 p. 10 = 2008 p. x). Crossref chapter records: Preface ix–xiv; Introduction to the 2008 Edition 1–14; 1. Introduction 15–33; Notes 168–201. Snippet wording used: 'Following but modifying Foucault's approach, I take the term \"discourses\" to refer not to language as separate from the real world but rather to organized sets of signifying practices' (p. x); 'Moral regulation is very closely linked to state formation, as the British school of historical sociology has argued; but as Foucault and his followers have pointed out, the state has no monopoly on moral regulation' (p. 165); moral regulation 'might be an important social process, which is intertwined with but is not an effect of either the economic or the political' (p. 165); 'Marxist accounts (and in Canada this chiefly means accounts produced by the political economy school)' (p. 165); 'one has to be sceptical of any claims that envision a clear separation between moral and economic regulation' (p. 166); n. 33 (p. 171) cites Valverde and Weir 1988; n. 34 (p. 171): in The Great Arch 'the role of the state in moral regulation is highlighted, and their theorization has been influential here; but the agencies of regulation internal to civil society are obscured'; n. 35 (p. 171) cites Bruce Curtis, 'Preconditions of the Canadian State'; p. 173: Corrigan and Sayer 'do not differentiate between moral and other modes of social regulation; by contrast, I restrict the term to mean the formation of ethical subjectivity'. The words before 'the term \"moral regulation\" from' on p. 173 were not visible. Acknowledgements (p. viii, snippet): 'Lorna Weir has been my best critic'."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/creighton/entry-draft.json:5: "review_status": "needs_review",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/creighton/entry-draft.json:52:   "evidence_note": "In-period, his own text. Dominion of the North (1944; pages read in an open copy): the post at Quebec gave the French 'the one great river system which led to the Atlantic seaboard from the heart of the continent'; 'Inevitably the river came to seem the natural basis of a great commercial empire stretching far into the distant west; and the achievement of this empire became the common objective of successive generations of Canadians, though their methods altered and the staple products of their trade were sometimes changed. The first staple of the St. Lawrence commercial system was beaver fur' (p. 21); the rivalry of 'the northern and southern routes, between Montreal and New York, for the control of the Great Lakes system and the command of the western trade', a conflict that 'was to remain essentially the same' (pp. 24–25); 'Quebec had been the origin of the empire of the St. Lawrence; it was to be the genesis of the Dominion of Canada' (p. 298). In-period reception. Herbert Heaton's review (CJEPS, 1938; first page, p. 565, read) quotes the book as 'an attempt to trace the relations between the commercial system of the St. Lawrence and the political development of Canada' (the book's words as Heaton quotes them; the 1937 book is unread) and reads it as a test of the economic interpretation of history that 'has pleased neither the Marxians nor their enemies'. Michel Brunet (RHAF, 1953, p. 579) calls him 'l'un des maîtres contemporains de l'historiographie anglo-canadienne'; Guy Frégault (RHAF, 1955, p. 446) sums up the Macdonald biography as 'Nationalisme antiaméricain, impérialisme et centralisation : synthèse canadienne, — je veux dire anglo-canadienne'. His own retrospect, 1969 (read in full; a later statement by the author, not evidence of what the 1937 book says): 'This Laurentian theme has its basis in the fact that the St. Lawrence is the one great river system that leads from the Atlantic Seaboard to the heart of the continent of North America'; the river 'has inspired generations of Canadians to build a great territorial empire, both commercial and political, in the western interior of the continent' (p. 16); 'The west, it was believed, would make Canada a nation, and the east-west axis would be its backbone' (p. 17); in choosing Macdonald he had 'stumbled upon the only satisfactory method of writing the second phase', so that he had written 'a trilogy on one theme' (p. 16). The phrase, he says, came to be regarded 'as the inspiration, at least in part, of a new school of Canadian historians and as the genesis of a new historical doctrine, the Laurentian interpretation of Canadian history'; he had 'no wish to disavow the parentage of an idea' (p. 14). Later readings: Ouellet (1984, pp. 226–227) calls him 'le chef de l'Ecole laurentienne'; Salmon (1997) says he 'simplified and expanded Innis' thesis'. Contested in period: Brunet found French-Canadian newspapers unread ('Il n'a pas jugé nécessaire de dépouiller un seul journal canadien-français', p. 580) and 'nombreux préjugés' (p. 581); Frégault asked 'où sont les Canadiens français dans tout cela ? Tout simplement, ils n'y sont pas' (p. 447), while praising the work as 'fondé sur une compréhension exacte de l'histoire du Canada' (p. 444); W. L. Morton, as quoted by Davis (1985, p. 97), saw in Creighton 'the preponderant role commercial monopoly and centralized business have played' and held that the metropolitan thesis, which he associated with Creighton and Careless, 'seems necessarily to minimize the importance of the hinterland'; the critic George Woodcock (1979, p. 140) called his treatment of Confederation as centralist 'a major distortion'. A contribution, not a founding.",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/creighton/entry-draft.json:67:   "review_status": "needs_review",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/creighton/entry-draft.json:82:   "evidence_note": "His own texts. Dominion of the North (1944) frames the St. Lawrence system by its staples ('The first staple of the St. Lawrence commercial system was beaver fur', p. 21), and its reading list (pp. 505–507) names Innis's The Fur Trade in Canada (1930) and The Cod Fisheries (1940). The 1957 presidential address recalls 'the 1930's, when the influence of Harold Innis was at its height and everybody was writing and talking about staple production' (p. 3), and exempts Innis from the Marxian fashion: 'most of the professional economists — and especially Innis — refused to accept the simplified Marxian version of historical determinism' (p. 6). In 1957 he also published Harold Adams Innis: Portrait of a Scholar (unread); Benoît Brouillette's review (1957, p. 529, read) reports Creighton's account of Innis's Canada as an east–west system 'marqué par le Saint-Laurent et les Grands-Lacs', at once 'transocéanique et transcontinental'. The qualification, his own (1969, pp. 14–15): 'The Empire of the St. Lawrence, it is sometimes confidently asserted, was the fruit of my friendship with Harold Innis ... These explanations, like most simple generalizations, happen to be wrong'; the subject was suggested 'not by Harold Innis, whom I barely knew at the time, but by W. P. M. Kennedy', and he was 'reluctantly convinced by the weight of his own evidence' about the Montreal merchants (p. 16). Readers: George Woodcock (1979, p. 134): 'Creighton's master was H. A. Innis, especially the Innis of The Fur Trade in Canada, which contained the essential germ out of which — with Innis's encouragement — Creighton built the scenario of his first book'; Fernand Ouellet (1984, p. 226): Creighton started 'partant de la théorie des staples'; Deborah Harrison (1981), as quoted by Davis (1985, p. 97): 'probably the first to advance a metropolitanist interpretation, although the closely related \"staples\" thesis had been put forward by Innis much earlier'; Salmon (1997): he 'simplified and expanded Innis' thesis'. The edge records Innis's work as a resource the Laurentian narrative used, not a teacher–pupil derivation; Creighton's denial concerns the origin of the subject, not the use of the staple framework.",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/creighton/entry-draft.json:97:   "review_status": "needs_review",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/creighton/entry-draft.json:120:   "review_status": "needs_review",
