data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/creighton/entry-draft.json:165:   "reason": "Not proposed. Davis (1985, p. 97) counts him among the 'deans' of five metropolitan approaches and quotes Harrison that he was 'probably the first to advance a metropolitanist interpretation'; the metropolitanism strand of cdn_national_tradition belongs to Careless (drafted separately), and edge_800 (urbanhistory → cdn_national_tradition, critique) already records urban historians' rejection of the metropolitan thesis. Davis reports Careless saying he drew 'neither from Creighton or Innis' (p. 101), so no Creighton → Careless arrow is proposed either."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/creighton/entry-draft.json:271:   "scope_note": "In-period French-Canadian review of the second Macdonald volume: praise with a critique of its anglo-Canadian synthesis."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/careless/entry-draft.json:5: "review_status": "needs_review",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/careless/entry-draft.json:29:  "scope_note": "This entry rests on texts read on 2026-10-14: the 1954 article in full (Canadian Historical Review 35:1, pp. 1–21, in an Internet Archive scan of the issue); the 1979 Jackson lecture 'Limited Identities — Ten Years Later' in full (Manitoba History no. 1, 1981, web text without page numbers); Davis (Urban History Review, 1985, pp. 95–113) in full; Szostak's review of Frontier and Metropolis (Journal of Economic History, 1990, pp. 499–501) in full; and Buckner (Acadiensis, 2000), pp. 4–10. The 1969 article is unread here: its wording is taken from Buckner's quotations and from Careless's own quotation of its last page in 1981. Frontier and Metropolis and Brown of the Globe are unread (catalogue records; the book's content through Szostak's review). He is not presented as the originator of either idea. In 1954 he credits metropolitanism to Lower, to Masters's Rise of Toronto (1947) and its source in N. S. B. Gras, and to the Laurentian school of Innis and Creighton (pp. 16–17); Davis (1985, p. 97) notes that Lower discussed metropolitanism 'some eight years before'. He wrote in 1981 that he 'did not invent the term “limited identity”' but acquired it from Ramsay Cook, so that his 1969 article 'was thus essentially a commentary on his initial idea'; Cook's coinage belongs to Cook's entry. Contested in period: by W. L. Morton and S. D. Clark (both as quoted by Davis), by Davis (1985), by Szostak (1990), by Irwin (1997, abstract) and by Buckner (2000). Michael Bliss (1991–92) is not recorded as a critic of Careless: Buckner (2000, p. 9) pairs Bliss's fragmentation charge with Careless's own later worry."
