data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/careless/entry-draft.json:164:   "reason": "He calls metropolitanism 'at root a socio-economic concept' (p. 17) and links it to Innis's staple studies (p. 16), but edge_797 (cdn_national_tradition → economic) carries the staple approach, and Davis (1985, p. 97) says the staples thesis 'did not become an integral part of metropolitanist thought until recently'. Szostak (1990) reviewed Frontier and Metropolis in the Journal of Economic History but judged it 'descriptive rather than analytical'; that is reception, not a contribution to economic history."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/careless/entry-draft.json:184:   "reason": "Irwin (1997, abstract) proposes environment as the ground of prairie identity against the metropolitan thesis; that is a critique of Careless, not his contribution. In 1954 'environmentalism' means frontier environmentalism (pp. 5–6), not environmental history."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/careless/entry-draft.json:190:   "review_status": "needs_review",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/careless/entry-draft.json:196:    "evidence_note": "Buckner (2000, p. 5) calls the pre-1960s Canadian approach \"similar to that of the consensus school in the United States\": minimizing internal divisions and stressing what united Canadians. Davis (1985, p. 102) likewise says Careless's metropolitan interpretation \"fits the mould of 'consensus' history\". Comparison with the consensus half of the atlas entry only. Careless's 1954 article, read for revision 1.135, criticises the Canadian historians who adapted Turner's frontier thesis; that critique is the separate edge from his person entry (j_m_s_careless → progressive), not this comparison.",
