data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/batch.json:32:   "evidence_note": "Buckner (2000, p. 5) calls the pre-1960s Canadian approach \"similar to that of the consensus school in the United States\": minimizing internal divisions and stressing what united Canadians. Davis (1985, p. 102) likewise says Careless's metropolitan interpretation \"fits the mould of 'consensus' history\". Comparison with the consensus half of the atlas entry only. Careless's 1954 article, read for revision 1.135, criticises the Canadian historians who adapted Turner's frontier thesis; that critique is the separate edge from his person entry (j_m_s_careless → progressive), not this comparison.",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/batch.json:40:   "evidence_note": "Ruonavaara's abstract describes Corrigan and Sayer's framework as fusing Durkheimian and Foucauldian analysis with basic Marxist theory; Dean's abstract argues that problems in Corrigan's account are better formulated through Foucault's problematic of governmentality. Drawn as a contribution, the atlas's usual kind for Foucault's outgoing edges. Valverde's own acknowledgement (checked 2026-10-14 for revision 1.135, in Google Books search-within snippets of the 2008 reissue, 1991 pagination): 'Following but modifying Foucault's approach, I take the term \"discourses\" to refer not to language as separate from the real world but rather to organized sets of signifying practices' (preface, p. x) and 'as Foucault and his followers have pointed out, the state has no monopoly on moral regulation' (p. 165; also quoted by Dehli 1992, p. 317). Wright (Acadiensis, 1995, p. 123, read) reads her as modifying 'Foucault's archaeology' with 'an important commitment to social agency'. Strange and Loo (1997, p. 5, snippet): 'Theorists influenced by philosopher Michel Foucault have urged scholars to abandon the social-control model for a more flexible concept of regulation'. Not a claim about every Canadian study in the roster.",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/batch.json:50:   "evidence_note": "Valverde studies the moral fervour of Protestant social reformers (publisher description); the religion-and-reform entry studies the same churches and social gospel as religious history (Christie and Gauvreau 1996, title-level). The two fields read the reformers differently. In period, Loo (Labour/Le Travail 30, 1992, p. 278, read) asked Valverde's book to 'address Christianity and certainly Protestantism more fully, even if only to dismiss its importance', and Christie and Gauvreau (1996, p. xiii, Google Books snippet) said she and Doug Owram used 'a caricature of the antiquated and esoteric Victorian theologue to excise the churches'. Those objections concern one book and are recorded on the edge from the mariana_valverde entry (revision 1.135); this field-level edge stays a comparison. PhD context: 67 of 164 2009–2026 PhDs engaging Moral regulation also engage Religion, social reform & secularization in Canada (41%; lift 3.97), counted after removing shared anchors. Bibliographies record consultation, not influence; lifts are high for most Canadian pairs. Valverde places moral reform at the centre of English Canada's race, class and gender order; Cook's Regenerators covers the same reformers' religious thought. PhD context: 25 of 42 PhDs citing The Regenerators also cite Valverde (lift 7.2). Comparison only.",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/release/editorial-additions.json:90:   "scope_note": "This entry rests on texts read on 2026-10-14: the 1954 article in full (Canadian Historical Review 35:1, pp. 1–21, in an Internet Archive scan of the issue); the 1979 Jackson lecture 'Limited Identities — Ten Years Later' in full (Manitoba History no. 1, 1981, web text without page numbers); Davis (Urban History Review, 1985, pp. 95–113) in full; Szostak's review of Frontier and Metropolis (Journal of Economic History, 1990, pp. 499–501) in full; and Buckner (Acadiensis, 2000), pp. 4–10. The 1969 article is unread here: its wording is taken from Buckner's quotations and from Careless's own quotation of its last page in 1981. Frontier and Metropolis and Brown of the Globe are unread (catalogue records; the book's content through Szostak's review). He is not presented as the originator of either idea. In 1954 he credits metropolitanism to Lower, to Masters's Rise of Toronto (1947) and its source in N. S. B. Gras, and to the Laurentian school of Innis and Creighton (pp. 16–17); Davis (1985, p. 97) notes that Lower discussed metropolitanism 'some eight years before'. He wrote in 1981 that he 'did not invent the term “limited identity”' but acquired it from Ramsay Cook, so that his 1969 article 'was thus essentially a commentary on his initial idea'; Cook's coinage belongs to Cook's entry. Contested in period: by W. L. Morton and S. D. Clark (both as quoted by Davis), by Davis (1985), by Szostak (1990), by Irwin (1997, abstract) and by Buckner (2000). Michael Bliss (1991–92) is not recorded as a critic of Careless: Buckner (2000, p. 9) pairs Bliss's fragmentation charge with Careless's own later worry."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/release/editorial-additions.json:163:   "scope_note": "Her own words are read in 'Nature and Hierarchy' (Atlantis, 1985, pp. 39–44, in full), the first-page extract of the 1988 CSSH article and the abstract of the 1999 ILWCH article. Her books are not read here: The Gender of Breadwinners, Domestic Goods and Sensing Changes are quoted only as their reviewers quote them, with the reviewers' page references, and the 1995 CHR essay only as Iacovetta and Kealey (1996) and Sangster (1996) quote it. In-period reviews read in full: Drystek (1980), Heron (CHR, 1991), Guildford (Atlantis, 1991), Scheinberg (Archivaria, 1991), Dennis (EcHR, 1991), Bertaux (JEH, 1992), Benson (JAH, 1993), Brush (EH.NET, 2000), Owram (BC Studies, 2001); the four Left History papers of 1995–96 are read in full. She is not presented as a founder: her 1985 essay places itself among 'a good many others' asking for the change, and the Canadian gender-history debate also turned on Pierson (1991) and Scott. The 1995 CHR essay appeared in an issue of historiographical essays, not in a CHR exchange; the exchange that took it up was in Left History. The environmental work dates from 2006–10 and is used only for the environment edge; a 2026 CHR forum on The Gender of Breadwinners is a later reading."
