data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/release/editorial-additions.json:828:   "evidence_note": "Her own in-period statements. 'Pauperism, Moral Character, and the Liberal State', Labour/Le Travail 30 (1992), pp. 213–222, read in full: 'I have argued elsewhere that the discourse of moral reform needs to be analyzed seriously, not merely dismissed as a cover for class interests', though 'moral discourse does as a matter of contingent fact legitimize certain class, gender, and racial interests' (p. 216); she credits Corrigan and Sayer's The Great Arch with pioneering 'the liberal state's role in constituting subjectivities' (p. 217). The Age of Light, Soap, and Water (1991) is known here through in-period reviewers' quotations and through Google Books search-within snippets of the 2008 University of Toronto Press reissue (volume ghqEyXf5950C; partial preview, page images not viewable here because the viewing limit was reached), which keep the 1991 pagination: reformers sought 'to reshape the ethical subjectivity of both immigrants and native-born Canadians' (p. 17; quoted by Dehli 1992, p. 316); purity work 'was not simply a question of banning obscene books or suppressing prostitution but was rather a campaign to educate the next generation' (p. 17; quoted by McCormick 1992, p. 124); the reform movements 'helped to shape the bourgeoisie, which led the movements, as well as the working class' (p. 15; Dehli, p. 318); 'Moral regulation is very closely linked to state formation, as the British school of historical sociology has argued; but as Foucault and his followers have pointed out, the state has no monopoly on moral regulation' (p. 165; snippet, the last clause also quoted by Dehli, p. 317); '[m]oral regulation is an important aspect of ruling, helping to constitute class, gender, sexual, and race relations' (p. 166, as quoted by Wright 1995, p. 124). Her notes, seen only as snippets, place the concept: in The Great Arch 'the role of the state in moral regulation is highlighted, and their theorization has been influential here; but the agencies of regulation internal to civil society are obscured' (n. 34, p. 171); Corrigan and Sayer 'do not differentiate between moral and other modes of social regulation; by contrast, I restrict the term to mean the formation of ethical subjectivity' (p. 173). Note 33 (p. 171) cites the 1988 article with Lorna Weir, which is unread here. In-period reception by historians. Tina Loo (Labour/Le Travail 30, 1992, pp. 277–279, read) calls the book 'a provocative and valuable contribution to the history of social reform and, more significantly, because of its self-conscious use of theory, to the writing of Canadian history' (p. 279). Donald Wright (Acadiensis, 1995, pp. 122–126 read) calls it a 'much talked about treatise' by 'a sociologist' (p. 122) and its concept of 'slippages' among class, gender, sexuality and race her 'most original' contribution (p. 124). Carolyn Strange and Tina Loo's Making Good: Law and Moral Regulation in Canada (1997; Google Books snippets, pp. 5, 63) write that 'Theorists influenced by philosopher Michel Foucault have urged scholars to abandon the social-control model for a more flexible concept of regulation' (p. 5) and cite 'Mariana Valverde and others' on prostitution as 'the social evil' (p. 63). Mary Louise Adams's 'In Sickness and in Health: State Formation, Moral Regulation, and Early VD Initiatives in Ontario' (1994) is cited at title level only. In-period criticism. Loo finds the second argument, on philanthropy and the state, 'more asserted than proved' (p. 279). Kari Dehli (Historical Studies in Education, 1992, pp. 315–319, read) would draw 'not quite so sharply drawn' conclusions about state and civil society and finds that she moves 'quickly from analysis of discursive strategies ... to drawing conclusions about people's motives and intentions' (pp. 318–319). Wright judges her claim that audiences 'knew precisely what was meant' 'untenable' and finds 'the impoverished immigrant and the \"fallen woman\" ... conspicuously silent', so that 'the silence of the consumer amidst the din of the producer gives the impression of top-down social control, precisely what Valverde sought to avoid' (pp. 125–126). Chris McCormick (Atlantis, 1992, pp. 123–125, read) asks how her interpretations are made and judges that a 'thorough' reading of context is 'optimistic' (p. 123), while agreeing that 'Regulation is a better word than control' (p. 124). Sociologists reformulated the concept: Alan Hunt's 1997 abstract (read) treats moral regulation not as 'a specific moral arena' but as 'a component of all fields of social relations', a wider definition than hers; in Governing Morals (1999; snippets) he writes 'I do not take issue with Valverde's invocation of ethical subjectivity, but I emphasise the mundane features of self-governing' (p. 155). Dean (1994) and Ruonavaara (1997) are cited through their abstracts as on the moral_regulation node. A contribution, not a founding: Corrigan (1981) and Corrigan and Sayer (1985) precede her, and she credits them. Diseases of the Will (1998), on alcohol and the governance of the will, is title-level here (snippets only) and does not support this edge.",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/release/editorial-additions.json:1167:   "scope_note": "In-period French-Canadian review of the second Macdonald volume: praise with a critique of its anglo-Canadian synthesis."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/release/editorial-additions.json:1549:    "note": "Read in full (pp. 213–222) in the journal's own PDF (lltjournal.ca article 4860, galley 5733; citation line on p. 213: Labour/Le Travail, 30 (Fall 1992), 213-22). Passages used: 'a critique of political economy for its failure to theorize moral regulation and subjectivity generally' (pp. 213–214); 'moral capital of the poor' (p. 215); 'the discourse of moral reform needs to be analyzed seriously, not merely dismissed as a cover for class interests' and 'moral discourse does as a matter of contingent fact legitimize certain class, gender, and racial interests' (p. 216); Dean shows 'that classical English liberalism was not a negation of state regulation but rather a particular system of economic, social, and moral regulation'; 'a history of pauperism is also a genealogy of liberalism'; Dean 'undercuts the Marxist view that moral regulation is an unimportant offshoot of economic class relations by expanding the analysis of the liberal state's role in constituting subjectivities pioneered by Corrigan and Sayer' (p. 217); Fecteau's study 'sadly lacking' Dean's awareness of gender (p. 220); n. 10 on Fecteau's essay in Colonial Leviathan and his perspective 'not to my knowledge otherwise represented in Canadian historiography' (p. 220); 'moyens de contrôle', 'a rather functionalist formulation', 'a model of negative class power', 'the pitfalls of marxist functionalism', 'These moral codes are not intrinsic to the logic of capital', 'a landmark work' (p. 222); thanks to Lynne Marks and Allan Greer (p. 222)."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/release/editorial-additions.json:1638:   "note": "Addendum 2026-10-14 (Claude, Parr draft): read in full in an Internet Archive capture (15 February 2020) of the journal PDF (article/download/6970/6154). Pages 239–48 by running head. In n. 2 (p. 240) she quotes Parr's 'Gender History and Historical Practice' (in Parr and Rosenfeld, eds., Gender and History in Canada, 1996, p. 11) and says it tends 'to locate critics of gender history, feminist and anti-feminist alike, in the same oppositional group'. In n. 3 (p. 240) she notes she was reproached for not offering 'more criticism of historians such as Mariana Valverde and Joy Parr'. On p. 244 she quotes Parr (p. 14) that the move to gender 'began from the post-structuralist premise that identities were made in relationships', adding that the two 'have blossomed in tandem'. She concedes she had 'congealed and possibly blurred two issues' (p. 244). She questions 'the prevailing assumption that gender history as the field, post-structuralism as the method, equals a superior, more broad-minded history' (p. 246; n. 14 cites Parr p. 21)."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/release/editorial-additions.json:1643:   "note": "For the 1.135 person entry. The book's pages were not read. Wording is known from (a) in-period reviewers' quotations, read: Dehli 1992 (pp. 15, 17, 165), McCormick 1992 (pp. 9, 17, 42, 43, 90), Loo 1992 (pp. 35, 36, 41, 164), Wright 1995 (pp. 9–13, 41, 42, 43, 89–90, 98, 103, 166); and (b) Google Books search-within snippets of the 2008 UTP reissue (volume ghqEyXf5950C), whose page images were not viewable. The reissue keeps the 1991 pagination for the original text: the snippets put the reviewers' pp. 15, 17, 165 and 166 on the same pages, and the 1991 preface's p. 9 at p. ix (so 1991 p. 10 = 2008 p. x). Crossref chapter records: Preface ix–xiv; Introduction to the 2008 Edition 1–14; 1. Introduction 15–33; Notes 168–201. Snippet wording used: 'Following but modifying Foucault's approach, I take the term \"discourses\" to refer not to language as separate from the real world but rather to organized sets of signifying practices' (p. x); 'Moral regulation is very closely linked to state formation, as the British school of historical sociology has argued; but as Foucault and his followers have pointed out, the state has no monopoly on moral regulation' (p. 165); moral regulation 'might be an important social process, which is intertwined with but is not an effect of either the economic or the political' (p. 165); 'Marxist accounts (and in Canada this chiefly means accounts produced by the political economy school)' (p. 165); 'one has to be sceptical of any claims that envision a clear separation between moral and economic regulation' (p. 166); n. 33 (p. 171) cites Valverde and Weir 1988; n. 34 (p. 171): in The Great Arch 'the role of the state in moral regulation is highlighted, and their theorization has been influential here; but the agencies of regulation internal to civil society are obscured'; n. 35 (p. 171) cites Bruce Curtis, 'Preconditions of the Canadian State'; p. 173: Corrigan and Sayer 'do not differentiate between moral and other modes of social regulation; by contrast, I restrict the term to mean the formation of ethical subjectivity'. The words before 'the term \"moral regulation\" from' on p. 173 were not visible. Acknowledgements (p. viii, snippet): 'Lorna Weir has been my best critic'."
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/release/editorial-additions.json:1694:   "evidence_note": "Buckner (2000, p. 5) calls the pre-1960s Canadian approach \"similar to that of the consensus school in the United States\": minimizing internal divisions and stressing what united Canadians. Davis (1985, p. 102) likewise says Careless's metropolitan interpretation \"fits the mould of 'consensus' history\". Comparison with the consensus half of the atlas entry only. Careless's 1954 article, read for revision 1.135, criticises the Canadian historians who adapted Turner's frontier thesis; that critique is the separate edge from his person entry (j_m_s_careless → progressive), not this comparison.",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/release/editorial-additions.json:1702:   "evidence_note": "Ruonavaara's abstract describes Corrigan and Sayer's framework as fusing Durkheimian and Foucauldian analysis with basic Marxist theory; Dean's abstract argues that problems in Corrigan's account are better formulated through Foucault's problematic of governmentality. Drawn as a contribution, the atlas's usual kind for Foucault's outgoing edges. Valverde's own acknowledgement (checked 2026-10-14 for revision 1.135, in Google Books search-within snippets of the 2008 reissue, 1991 pagination): 'Following but modifying Foucault's approach, I take the term \"discourses\" to refer not to language as separate from the real world but rather to organized sets of signifying practices' (preface, p. x) and 'as Foucault and his followers have pointed out, the state has no monopoly on moral regulation' (p. 165; also quoted by Dehli 1992, p. 317). Wright (Acadiensis, 1995, p. 123, read) reads her as modifying 'Foucault's archaeology' with 'an important commitment to social agency'. Strange and Loo (1997, p. 5, snippet): 'Theorists influenced by philosopher Michel Foucault have urged scholars to abandon the social-control model for a more flexible concept of regulation'. Not a claim about every Canadian study in the roster.",
data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/release/editorial-additions.json:1712:   "evidence_note": "Valverde studies the moral fervour of Protestant social reformers (publisher description); the religion-and-reform entry studies the same churches and social gospel as religious history (Christie and Gauvreau 1996, title-level). The two fields read the reformers differently. In period, Loo (Labour/Le Travail 30, 1992, p. 278, read) asked Valverde's book to 'address Christianity and certainly Protestantism more fully, even if only to dismiss its importance', and Christie and Gauvreau (1996, p. xiii, Google Books snippet) said she and Doug Owram used 'a caricature of the antiquated and esoteric Victorian theologue to excise the churches'. Those objections concern one book and are recorded on the edge from the mariana_valverde entry (revision 1.135); this field-level edge stays a comparison. PhD context: 67 of 164 2009–2026 PhDs engaging Moral regulation also engage Religion, social reform & secularization in Canada (41%; lift 3.97), counted after removing shared anchors. Bibliographies record consultation, not influence; lifts are high for most Canadian pairs. Valverde places moral reform at the centre of English Canada's race, class and gender order; Cook's Regenerators covers the same reformers' religious thought. PhD context: 25 of 42 PhDs citing The Regenerators also cite Valverde (lift 7.2). Comparison only.",
