data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-2026-10-14/parr/entry-draft.json:483:  "cdnsrc_sangster_1996": "Addendum 2026-10-14 (Claude, Parr draft): read in full in an Internet Archive capture (15 February 2020) of the journal PDF (article/download/6970/6154). Pages 239–48 by running head. In n. 2 (p. 240) she quotes Parr's 'Gender History and Historical Practice' (in Parr and Rosenfeld, eds., Gender and History in Canada, 1996, p. 11) and says it tends 'to locate critics of gender history, feminist and anti-feminist alike, in the same oppositional group'. In n. 3 (p. 240) she notes she was reproached for not offering 'more criticism of historians such as Mariana Valverde and Joy Parr'. On p. 244 she quotes Parr (p. 14) that the move to gender 'began from the post-structuralist premise that identities were made in relationships', adding that the two 'have blossomed in tandem'. She concedes she had 'congealed and possibly blurred two issues' (p. 244). She questions 'the prevailing assumption that gender history as the field, post-structuralism as the method, equals a superior, more broad-minded history' (p. 246; n. 14 cites Parr p. 21)."

